## Nomenclature
## 1. Introduction – background and scope of review
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers (PCPs) are infinitely extending networks built from coordination bonds which have potential voids [1]. The genesis of MOFs has relied on coordination chemistry which has developed substantially since it discovery. Although the extension of coordination bonded entities as coordination polymers was proposed as early as in 1960s [2,3], the field received remarkable propulsion only after the seminal contributions in the late 1980 s and 1990 s which carved a niche for MOFs as a sub-domain of coordination polymers, on the basis of compounds bearing and retaining porosity [4–10]. Largely till the turn of the millennium, the research in this field was focused on cyanide terminal linkers or pyridyl-containing ligands, with the charge balance equilibration left to the ionic components of the reaction mixture [11–29]. Upon the development of carboxylate-based linkers, which led to synthesis of stable frameworks, an area of exploration in terms of porous properties was accentuated [30–35]. Subsequently the formation of extended porous architectures based on azolates was investigated [36–42]. Owing to the higher donating strength of azolates, frameworks formed out of these linkers provided a feasible pathway to develop robust porous solids. Around the same period, linkers based on other donor groups such as sulfonates, phosphonates, hydroxyl etc. too received attention [43–52].
Thus reviewing the different class of MOFs, with regard to building blocks, in the context of recent advancements is of high relevance. Several reviews have tracked the progress of the field [53–56], which have found the utility of carboxylate-based linkers and N-donor ligands as two major contributors of organic building blocks in MOFs. N-donor linkers broadly constitute two distinct types viz. charged and neutral donor linkers, with each type offering characteristic features [57–59]. The chemistry of charged ligand-based MOFs has evolved rapidly over the years and been surveyed consistently in literature [60–67], and hence more attention has been devoted to reports of MOFs constructed out of neutral N-donor ligands in the present review. The motivation of the review is to provide a blended perspective on N-donor linker (charged and neutral donor) based MOFs. Also given the extensive growth in the field of MOF, instead of a detailed survey we have focused on the advancements in the field and aimed to provide the reader a flavor of the recent emerging applications. To have coherent outlook we have narrowed the scope of the review to porous systems built from N-donor based linkers, unless specifically required, and excluded compounds constructed from other kinds of donor groups (carboxylates, sulfonates, phosphonates etc.) or non-porous coordination polymers and mixed linker based MOFs.
## 2. Structural features of N-donor linker based MOFs
## 2.1. Charged N-donor linker based MOFs
## 2.1.1. Classification and structural features
Charged N-donor linkers, typically azolates, have been commonly employed for the fabrication of porous coordination polymers [39,40,57,68–70]. This class of linker constitutes five membered heterocycle azolate and the coordination mode is via $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ N-donor atoms upon deprotonation. In this class of framework, fine tuning of the thermal and chemical stability via linker modulation is a major area of exploration that has commanded significant research attention [71]. Investigation of azolate based frameworks has gained significant research interest due to the features associated with these frameworks which are vital from the practical standpoint. Azolate based linkers are known for their high reactivity resulting in formation of insoluble powders and obtaining single crystals requires specific protocols. In recent years, large scale exploration has laid out guidelines for obtaining highly crystalline solids from these ligands. Depending on the number of nitrogen atoms in the heterocycle, the azolate based frameworks can be classified as discussed below. Metal azolate based frameworks are fabricated from five membered heterocycle containing variable number of nitrogen donor atoms (Scheme 1). This class of framework constitutes azole moieties varying from imidazole that contains two ligating nitrogen atom to tetrazole that contains four coordinating nitrogen atoms, which deprotonate to form azolate anion. More recently, the pentazole anion has been isolated and initial studies have emerged wherein fabrication of frameworks utilizing pentazole anion have emerged [72]. Increasing the number N-atoms give rise to increase in acidity of the linker donor groups. Different linkers offer variable coordination modes and angles for example imidazolate anion offers an angle of $140^{\circ}$ in contrast to shorter binding angle of pyrazolate i.e. $.70^{\circ}$ . The binding modes and geometry for azolates can be predicted with an ease if all the ligating sites are availed (energy minima). Most of the azolate react with univalent coinage metals viz. $\mathsf{A g}(\mathrm{I})$ and $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ and first row transition metals like $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , $\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II}),\mathsf{C o}(\mathrm{II})$ etc. Azolate derived frameworks render simple geometry yet rich diversity of structural topologies are possible. The diversity of self-assembled structures is observed as the binding modes are positioned at angles other than conventionally observed for 6-membered systems such as $180^{\circ}$ , $120^{\circ}$ or $60^{\circ}$ . Although single azolate units can themselves lead to formation of polymeric networks, there have been several linkers constructed having two or more such terminal donor groups such as 1,4-di(1H-imidazol-4-yl)benzene, leading to formation of porous MOFs [73–75].
Azolate based N-donor linkers are known to form several cluster based compounds owing to the similar binding modes as carboxylate based linkers. Pyrazolates result in formation of secondary building units (SBUs) based frameworks among which few well known SBUs are as follows: cubic $\mathrm{\small{[M_{8}(\mu_{4}{-}O H)_{6}(\mu{-}P Z)_{12}]}}$ octahedral $[\mathrm{M}_{4}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-O})(\upmu\mathrm{-P}Z)_{6}]$ ; triangular $[\mathrm{M}_{3}(\upmu_{3}{-}0)(\upmu{-}\mathrm{PZ})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]^{+}$ triple zigzag chain with octahedral metal ions; double zigzag chain with square-planar metal ions; double zigzag chain with tetrahedral metal ions. Cluster formation leads to enhanced chemical and thermal stability. In few cases, pyrazolate based network form SBUs viz. $[\mathsf{M}_{3}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{P}Z)_{3}]$ which are quite known for their chemical stability due to increased hydrophobicity. As the binding angles are acute, the extension of the network is generally via increasing denticity, unlike imidazolates, wherein only one single unit can lead to formation of higher dimensional structures. Triazolate based linkers bind similar to pyrazolate, but with the additional possibility of 3rd coordination site. The regular geometry of 1,2,4-triazolate is Y shaped which deviates from the regular triangle thus resulting in several new topologies. On the other hand, tetrazole derived linkers coordinate with different number of N-atoms from one, two, three or four, which leads to unique structures and topologies. Importantly these donor groups can also be derived insitu thus facilitating fabrication of azolate structures [64].
MOFs are broadly classified into neutral (nMOFs) and ionic (iMOFs), based on charge of the framework backbone. Ionic MOFs (iMOFs) are subdivided into cationic (iMOF-C) and anionic (iMOF-A) (Scheme 2) [124]. Azolate based linkers may give neutral as well as ionic framework depending on the connectivity of the linker, metal coordination environment and coordination geometry. Majority of the systems reported from the use of azolate based
