linkers have been found to be neutral in nature, wherein the charge balance is either via anionic donor groups of azolates or the presence of coordinating anion used in the synthesis (Fig. 1). This is valid for the different kinds of azolates, for instance all the ZIF-series MOFs which are constructed from imidazolates are electrically neutral [57]. Likewise pyrazolate and triazolate based systems too have been found to self-assemble with metal ions/clusters into neutral MOFs [76,77].
However, in certain cases due to the varied binding modes of the linker and anion or unusual coordination environment of the metal node, leads to formation of ionic MOFs (iMOFs), which bear presence of uncoordinated ions in the void spaces. Typically as azolate based linkers are ionic in nature, the formation of anionic MOFs (iMOF-A) is more commonly observed, for example Long and co-workers had reported the formation of anionic framework using tetrazolate as the donor linker in manganese-based MOF viz. $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{DMF})_{6}]_{3}[(\mathrm{MnCl})_{3}(\mathrm{BTT})_{8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{12}]_{2}.$ .42DMF $11\mathrm{H}_{2}020\mathrm{CH}_{3}0\mathrm{H}$ (btt 1,3,5-benzentristetrazolate) [78]. Recently in a report by Dinca and co-workers a copper-based MOF viz. MIT-20 $((\mathrm{CH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{NH_{2}})[\mathrm{Cu_{2}C l_{3}B T D D}].(\mathrm{DMF})_{4}(\mathrm{H_{2}O})_{4.5}$ was reported which had an anionic backbone [79]. The metal node exhibited square pyramidal coordination geometry and bridged via Cl-atoms. Although rare, but azolate based linkers can lead to formation of cationic MOFs (iMOF-C) too. In a report by Zhang and co-workers, a $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ based MOF viz. $[\mathrm{Zn}_{7}(\mathrm{ip})_{12}](\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ (MAF-34) (Hip- 1H-imidazo[4,5-f][1,100]phenanthroline) was found to bear presence of free hydroxide anions, due to unusual coordination brought about by the deprotonation of the Hip ligand [80].
As the donor atoms in the ring are closely spaced, the presence of substituents on the ligands directly affects formation of polymeric structures and influences porous properties more significantly. Another interesting facet of azolate-chemistry lies in the relative stability. When the $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ electrons are coordinated to metal ion, the ligand donor sites become saturated and are unavailable for any interaction. Thus unlike carboxylate donor groups, azolate-based systems tend to have local hydrophobic character, which not only enhances stability but also renders remarkable host–guest features. Azolate based frameworks can be superior to popularly employed carboxylate based linkers, as in the case of azolate the small heterocyclic coordinates directly to the metal center. This provides higher activation barrier for rotation unlike carboxylate based frameworks [81]. Although the flexible nature of such azolate based frameworks have been established [82,83], they yield interesting mechanical properties. These features (thermal and chemical stability) are quintessential for practical consideration in all frontiers viz. gas sorption, separation, fuel cell, energy related applications (representative examples are discussed in the subsequent sections). Majority of the MOF synthesized by this class of linkers have pores in the microporous domain $(<20\mathring\mathrm{A})$ .
## 2.2. Neutral N-donor linker based MOFs
## 2.2.1. Classification and structural features
Neutral N-donor terminal bi-/multi-dentate linkers offer a platform to form networks which leave equilibration of the electronic charge to other components of the reaction medium. Generally donor groups in such linkers vary from 6-membered heterocycles such as pyridine, pyrazine to 5-membered ring systems such as imidazole, pyrazole, triazole or tetrazole [54]. Some of the wellstudied donor groups have been depicted in Scheme 1, which have been part of several employed linkers such as $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine, 2,4,6-tri(4-pyridyl)-1,3,5-triazine, 1,4-bis(1-imidazolyl)benzene, 1,3,5-tris(1-imidazolyl)benzene, 1,3,5-tris(imidazol-1-ylmethyl)be nzene etc. [84–97]. It is noteworthy to mention that the formative years of MOF-chemistry was developed using neutral N-donor ligands [5–10]. The components influencing the self-assembly of the MOF formation are type of metal nodes, characteristic of the donor groups and binding strength of the inorganic anion. Typically, depending upon the coordination ability of the N-donor group and the anion employed in the reaction medium, the characteristic of the end product is determined (Fig. 2) [98,99].
For instance strong coordinating anions (e.g. $\mathsf{S C N^{-1}}$ ) bind to the metal nodes, whereas weakly coordinating anions such as $\mathsf{P F}_{6}^{-}$ , are likely to remain uncoordinated in the void spaces. In the latter case, the solvent molecules occupy the vacant coordinating sites, if any, and complete the coordination sphere.
Broadly, the end product obtained from utilizing such linkers can be classified into neutral or ionic (predominantly cationic). Most commonly the formation of cationic MOFs is achieved by employing neutral linkers with having higher binding tendencies or using weakly coordinating anions.
An overview of the different classes of compounds obtained from such linkers and the various strategies employed is discussed below.
2.2.1.1. Neutral MOFs (nMOFs). Formation of neutral MOF using neutral linkers involves coordination of counter-ions employed in the reaction to the metal node. Generally the choice of the anion directly influences the self-assembly and the consequent structural features. The early development of MOFs, in fact, was based on synthesis of neutral MOFs (nMOFs) built from neutral linkers such as $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine or nitrile-terminal ligands; wherein the charge balance was completed by the anion used in the metal salt of the reaction [5–10,55]. Although subsequently neutral N-donor ligands have been popularly employed as co-linkers to extend the network of metal-carboxylates [102,103], and more recently for metal-sulfonates [104–106], MOFs constructed out of such ligands and metal-centers connected via inorganic ions (especially multi-dentate anions) is receiving greater attention. The spatial advantage in terms of control over microporosity and modular design endows this class of MOFs with synthetic ease, higher control and fine-tuning of molecular scale functions. As discussed above, the predominant choice of linkers in this class involves six membered heterocycles, such as pyridine or pyrazine. Due to steric constraints of employing polypyridyl linkers, the occupation of six independent linkers around coordination sphere of transition metals is generally unlikely. This provides access to inclusion of anions, to both complete the coordination sphere and balance the electronic charge. If the geometry of the linker is symmetric, usage of bindentate anions can lead to extension of framework dimensionality and give rise to pillar-layered structures (Fig. 2). Also in case of higher dentate linkers with these terminal linkers, the neutral structures obtained can result in interesting structures (Fig. 3a) [107,108], in which the anion binds to the metal nodes.
Although six membered terminal linkers would be better suited for obtaining neutral structures [109–112], 5-membered terminal linkers too are known to yield neutral compounds, wherein the anion is directly coordinated to the metal centre (Fig. 3b)
[113–115]. The choice of building blocks in such systems and the higher density of coordinating groups around a metal node provide versatility with regard to parameters such as interpenetration and stability; although the binding nature is similar. Several coordinating anions have been proposed for the synthesis of such frameworks, such as $\mathsf{P F}_{6}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathsf{C r O}_{4}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}0_{7}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{TiF}_{6}^{2-}$ , $\mathsf{N b O F}_{5}^{2-}$ etc. [116–121]. The topical research interest in such kinds of systems aimed at specific targeted applications is discussed in the subsequent sections.
2.2.1.2. Ionic MOFs (iMOFs). When the coordination sites are occupied by neutral linkers, either fully or majorly, then in those cases the equilibration of the electronic charge is left to the anion used in the reaction medium [122,123]. Typically, such linkers render cationic MOFs, which is a subset of ionic-MOFs [124]. Although neutral ligands predominantly yield cationic MOFs (iMOF-C), there have been rare examples of forming anionic MOFs (iMOF-A) [125]. It is noteworthy to mention that the prospect of such ionic compounds for ion-exchange applications was reported long ago [126], and the utility of such compounds has been explored over the years [127]. These compounds bear the presence of exchangeable anions/cations in the framework voids, which can be sought for ion-exchange based applications [128–131]. Generally, use of such neutral linkers has been found to be the most facile route to prepare cationic MOFs, although there are few other pathways which have been proposed [124]. The formation of any coordination network is strongly influenced by the choice of the building blocks viz. coordination sphere and charge of the metal ion, and the symmetry and donating ability of the linker. In case of preparing ionic-MOFs, the coordination ability of the uncoordinated ion or the lack of it, also contributes substantially in the eventual self-assembly. For instance, weakly coordinating anions facilitate the synthesis of cationic coordination polymers as they have to compete with strongly coordinating linker groups and other guest molecules used in the reaction medium [132,133]. This approach was most routinely employed for preparing several cationic MOFs. Since the growing utilization of 5-member donor groups, an alternate pathway for the preparation of cationic MOFs has emerged. This involves affordance of high density of organic linkers around the metal nodes, despite of a rigid or flexible backbone, which keeps the anions employed in the reaction medium away from the coordination sphere of the metal node (Fig. 4) [134–136].
Among the prominent applications sought for cationic MOFs, ion-exchange based processes are highly desired; which typically demands the presence of stable sorbents. The detailed applications afforded using such linkers and factors governing the formation of stable systems are described in the subsequent sections.
## 3. Factors governing stability of N-donor linker based MOFs
The general considerations for new compound development have broadened over the discourse with more emphasis on practical aspects, such as hydrolytic and chemical stability, thermal stability, durability, scalability and cost effectiveness. In pursuit of adhering to these parameters during the development of MOFs as target specific functional solids, the choice of building blocks has assumed even greater importance and seeking greater research attention, especially for neutral N-donor linkers. In this regard the general consensus drawn from notable contributions provides a broad outline in terms of design approaches for preparing stable MOFs [71,137]. In addition several reviews have outlined the protocol to ascertain stability [138–140].
In general the protection of the coordination bond from influence of external species provides stability, either by improving the coordination bond strength, use of SBUs or attaching water repelling groups (Scheme 3). Although different MOFs have demonstrated resistance to hydrolysis, typically stability is often a combination of multiple factors. Some of the broad parameters governing stability in N-donor linker based MOFs have been discussed below.
## 3.1. Metal-ligand bond strength
Understanding metal-linker bond holds the key towards fabrication of thermodynamically/kinetically stable MOFs. Metallinker bond strength is governed by several factors enumerated as metal ion charge/size radius, donating linker atom charge and nature and $\mathbf{M}-\mathbf{L}$ bond strength. This feature is in accordance with the HSAB principles which emphasizes that alike nature atoms (in terms of hardness/softness) ensues stronger bonds. Generally, highly charged metal ion $(\mathrm{Mn}+\mathrm{e.g.~}\mathrm{Zr}^{4+},\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ ) bind favorably with hard donors atoms e.g. oxygen donor linkers whilst for MOFs constructed from neutral N-donor linkers prefer to bind with softer metal ions with large size/charge ratio thus leading to stronger bonds. These empirical rules have been directly observed in the field of MOFs wherein a classical example is Zeolitic Imidazolate Framework (ZIF-8) [40]. This framework pose exceptional stability in various media viz. water, basic media. Authors have attributed this feature to the bonding strength between $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and imidazolate linker in synergism to the increased linker density resulting in increased hydrophobicity thus imparting greater stability. Stability is commanded via metal-nitrogen bond strength which is directly influenced by $\mathsf{p K}_{\mathbf{a}}$ values of different azolates /charge of linker [63]. In nitrogen donor based linkers pertaining especially for azolate (charged N-donor based linker) this classification becomes relevant for understanding the binding behavior and the effect of different $\mathsf{p K}_{\mathbf{a}}$ to overall stability. Azolate based frameworks react with soft metal ions leading to formation of stable network via
HSAB principles In combination, higher $\mathsf{p K}_{\mathrm{a}}$ leads to stronger metal-nitrogen bonds as the corresponding charge density on the donating nitrogen atoms is higher, resulting in stronger bonds. This phenomenon was noted by Long and co-workers wherein they systematically fabricated a series of pyrazolate based MOFs by varying metal ions such as $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , $C o^{2+}$ etc. which were found to be highly chemically and thermally stable (Fig. 5a-5b) [141]. The reasoning can be ascertained on the basis of $\mathsf{p K}_{\mathrm{a}}$ values and binding strength, as compared to neutral N-donor based linker. For example, $\mathbf{M}-\mathbf{L}$ bond distance for Ni-N bond in pyrazine is $\sim2.1\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ [142] while for pyrazole based linkers the value decreases significantly to approx $\sim1.97\mathring{\mathsf{A}}-2.0\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ [143]. As pyrazole based linkers are more basic as compared to the neutral donor linkers (values for pKa are 0.37 and 19.8 for pyrazine and pyrazole respectively) [144], thus stronger coordination bonds are formed as compared to the neutral N-donor linkers. Typically higher $\mathsf{p K}_{\mathrm{a}}$ values for nitrogen deprotonation leads to higher stability but this rule is not valid for azolates posing high $\mathsf{N}/\mathsf{C}$ ratio which detonate upon impact.
Depending on the charge of the linker and the reaction conditions, the choice of metal ion becomes extremely relevant for synthesis of chemically stable structures. The principles that govern the stability of such complexes arise from coordination chemistry priniciples accompanied with surface hydrophobicity. The $\mathbf{M}-\mathbf{L}$ bond strength has a direct influence on the chemical stability of the structures thus choice of metal becomes extremely crucial viz. for example in literature, Ni(II) metal ion has resulted in structures which possess wide range pH stability. Long and co-workers had earlier demonstrated this facet of a pyrazolate based MOF which exhibited exceptionally high thermal and chemical resistance [141]. Recently Zhou and co-workers demonstrated the high resistance of azolate based MOFs towards strong basic media (Fig. 5c-5d) [145]. Another interesting principle which may play important role in the choice of metal involves the Irving Williams series. This series puts an order to randomization available during the choice of metal ion. wherein the strength of the coordination bonds formed by transition metal ions and N-donor linker has been deduced to vary as follows $\mathrm{Mn^{2+}<F e^{2+}<\Delta C o^{2+}<\Delta N i^{2+}<\Delta C u^{2+}>\Delta Z n^{2+}}$ . Although stability has become a crucial parameter for any MOF development, the choice of metal ions in the present case is limited as the linker donor group are soft in nature [71].
## 3.2. Structural factors
A good portion of research have been focused towards constructing novel structures and topologies as divergent structures can directly influence the performance as well as the stability of the material. Interpenetration simultaneously offers enhanced wall thickness in the frameworks and reduced pore size in MOFs, both of which provide improved thermal stability. Additionally, catenation, another type of interpenetration imparts enhanced stability to MOFs and affects their adsorption behavior. Long and co-workers reported the importance of ligand directed catenation towards enhanced stability in sodalite-type MOFs [146]. The authors discovered that catenation had remarkable influence on the framework stability toward collapse upon activation. They also managed to obtain an increased surface area of $1580~\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and improved hydrogen adsorption performance. In another work Du and coworkers synthesized a three-fold interpenetrating diamondoid MOF constructed with $\mathsf{C u}_{4}\mathrm{I}_{4}$ clusters showing high thermal stability [147]. Generally interpenetration in MOFs help in the enhancement of thermal stability but it also has pitfalls like reduced pore size and increased overall density in the framework.
An alternative way to stabilize metal-organic frameworks is via construction of multi-walled frameworks. In this regard, Long and co-workers have synthesized two hydrolytically stable MOFs with molecular formula of Zn(1,4-BDP)2DEF $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (1) and $Z\mathrm{n}(1,3{\-}{\mathrm{BDP}}){\cdot}0$ . 7DMF $0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ (3) $(1,3/4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{BDP}=1,3/4$ -benzenedi(4-pyrazolyl)) constructed from $^{1,3-}$ and 1,4-benzenedipyrazolate organic linkers Fig. 6. [77]. Both the MOFs illustrated good thermal stability up to $400^{\circ}\mathsf C$ and $450^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 1 and 3 respectively. Compound 3 displayed superior chemical stability as compared to compound 1. Compound 3 retained its structural integrity when treated in various harsh conditions and the high degree of robustness of the framework is attributed to its double-walled framework based architecture. Along this line of fabricating stable MOFs Bu and co-workers synthesized two isoreticular MOFs bearing octahedral-cage based double-walled framework [148]. The authors used two molecular building blocks (MBBs) of similar size but distinct rigidity to obtain two MOFs with ‘‘Russian-doll-type’’ double-walled framework having molecular formula $[\mathrm{M}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{2}(\mathrm{TPT})_{2}.$ $\cdot x\mathrm{G}\mathrm{l}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $\mathbf{\dot{M}=N i}$ for 1 and Co for 2; $\mathrm{TPT}=2,4,6-$ tris(4-pyridyl)-1,3,5- triazine; $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{L}=2,4,6$ -tris[1-(3-carboxylphenoxy)ylmethyl]mesity lene; $G=$ guest molecules). Both the MOFs showed good thermal stability (up to $300^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . Further, the Ni-MOF retained its structure upon desolvation which was confirmed from the PXRD experiment. The same group further contributed toward stabilizing metal-organic frameworks by synthesizing the very first 3D hetero-triple-walled MOF, $\{[\mathrm{CO_{6}L_{4}(T P T)_{2}(\mu_{3}{-}O H)_{2}}]\mathrm{Co(H_{2}O)_{6}{\cdot}\mathcal{X}G\}_{\mathrm{n}}}$ where $\mathrm{TPT}=2{,}4{,}6$ -tris(4-pyridyl)-1,3,5-triazine, $\mathrm{H}3\mathrm{L}=2{,}4{,}6$ -tris[1- (3-carboxylphenoxy)ylmethyl]mesitylene, $G=$ guest molecules [149]. This MOF was found to exhibit superior chemical and thermal stability compared to its double-walled and single-walled analogues $([\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathrm{L}_{2}(\mathrm{TPT})_{2}x\mathrm{G}]_{\mathrm{n}})$ and $[(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{S C N})_{2})_{3}(\mathsf{T P T})_{4}x\mathsf{G}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ respectively. The outstanding hydrolytic stability of the hetero-triple-walled MOF was confirmed from the PXRD experiment. The framework also showed good resistance toward common organic solvents was found to be intact even after guest removal. Additionally a high degree of thermal stability up to $350^{\circ}\mathsf C$
Higher connectivity of metal nodes and ligands can serve as an ideal platform to bestow significant stability in MOFs. Xiang and co-workers documented an ultrastable MOF namely FJU-66 $(\mathrm{[Cu_{6}(N D I)_{3}]{\cdot2D M F{\cdot6}M e O H{\cdot2}H_{2}O}}$ [FJU-66 S where $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{NDI}$ : 2,7-bis (3,5-dimethyl) dipyrazol-1,4,5,8-naphthalene-tetracarboxydii mide, S: solvent molecules]) [150]. The 3D MOF was built on octahedral $\lvert\mathsf{C u}_{6}(\mathsf{P z})_{6}\rvert$ clusters comprising of two $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{P}z)_{3}]$ trimer units via Cu-Cu contacts. FJU-66 illustrated unusually high thermal stability up to $803\mathrm{K}$ without losing its structure which was validated from TGA and variable-temperature powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis. The authors attributed this exceptional stability to the shorter intertrimer $\mathsf{C u...C u}$ distance,i.e, cuprophilicity. Additionally FJU-66 was found to be stable from pH ranging from 3 to 14 over $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ time period. In another work Volmer and co-workers reported MFU-1 (MFU stands for metal-organic framework UlmUniversity) with molecular formula $[C\mathsf{o}_{4}^{\mathrm{II}}0(\mathsf{b d p b})_{3}]$ (where ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{bdpb}}$ $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ ,4-bis[(3,5-dimethyl)-pyrazol-4-yl]benzene) bearing similar secondary building units (SBUs) of MOF-5 [151]. MFU-1 network contains $\{\mathsf{C o}_{4}\mathsf{O}\}$ units connected via bdpb ligands producing a noninterpenetrated framework. The thermal stability up to $270^{\circ}\mathsf C$ was observed from the VTPXRD patterns. Furthermore, this compound showed good stability in presence of water and other protic solvents. Bordiga and co-workers synthesized two extremely stable MOFs fabricated on the cubic $\mathrm{Ni}_{8}$ -clusters and bispyrazolate ligands [152].
## 3.3. Hydrophobicity
Hydrolytic stability of MOFs can be improved by enhancing the shield to protect the metal-ligand bond. One of the more advantageous approach involves increasing the hydrophobicity; either at the pore opening or internally. The former route will forbid the entry of water inside the pore, to protect the coordination bond. For the latter pathway, although water may enter the pore, the attack on metal centers is prevented. Generally, water adsorption experiments are performed to testify the formation of hydrophobic systems. This approach has been adopted to equip several benchmark compounds which are susceptible to degradation in moisture to be able to resist hydrolysis. Typically, this involves attachment of alkyl groups or use of fluorinated linkers [153]. Recently other approaches too have emerged to address the challenge of stability via increasing hydrophobicity of MOFs.
In case of N-donor linker based MOFs, a benchmark example of stable compound is that of ZIF-8 [40]. The compound retains its integrity in boiling water and even under harsh alkaline conditions. Apart from the presence of strong coordination bond between imidazolate and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ , the unusual stability was attributed to the presence of methyl groups on a small linker. The high concentration of such groups in the structure makes the surface hydrophobic and forbids the attack of water. More recently, Lively and coworkers delved deeper into this behavior and found that the particle size and surface exposed too had a vital role in the observed stability of ZIF-8 [154].
Omary and co-workers exemplified the effect of fluorinated linkers in affording hydrophobicity to MOFs [155,156]. The authors extended their study using fluorinated linker viz. 3,5-bis(trifluoro methyl)-1,2,4-triazolate $(\mathsf{T}\boldsymbol{z})$ and investigated the ability of the $\mathsf{A g}(\mathrm{I})$ based MOF viz. AgTz (referred as FMOF-1) to adsorb oil constituents, even in presence of water (Fig. 7a). Water adsorption experiments revealed strong hydrophobicity and this experiment was compared with zeolite-5A and BPL carbon (Fig. 7b). The strong repulsion to water was attributed to the presence of superhydrophobic pore opening. Barea, Navarro and co-workers shed more light on this approach by investigating hydrophobicity in a series of isoreticular MOFs by varying the pore chemistry in Ni(II) based MOFs viz. $\mathrm{[Ni_{8}(L)_{6}]}$ (Fig. 7c) [157]. While shorter linkers with no substitution were found to be degrade, dipyrazolate linkers with $-\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ or ${\mathsf{-C F}}_{3}$ substitutions enhanced hydrophobicity and subsequent stability of the networks. The water adsorption experiments suggested $[\mathrm{Ni}_{8}(\mathrm{L}_{5}{\bf-C F}_{3})_{6}]$ (where $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{5}\:-\:4,4^{\prime}$ -(benzene-1,4-diyldie thyne-2,1-diyl)bis(1H-pyrazole)) to outperform Blucher-101408 (commercially available activated carbon) in terms of hydrophobicity (Fig. 7d). Popov, Miljanic and co-workers too reported the formation of hydrophobic MOF using perfluorinated tetrazolate ligand [158]. The formation of hydrophobic MOFs even with linkers with longer lengths advocated the utility of fluorinated linkers. Another approach involves employing hydrophobic groups as coligands or binding moieties. For instance, in the square-grid series of SIFSIX compounds the utilization of fluorinated anions results in enhancing hydrophobicity and resistance to moisture [159,160]. Similar observations have been noted by several groups by employing fluorinated linkers [161].
In case of neutral donor linkers, owing to relative lesser number of connecting sites, the denticity of the ligand becomes important for shielding the coordination bonds. For instance, shorter linkers such as imidazole (5-membered) against pyridyl (6-membered) would be more feasible for increasing concentration around metal nodes and sterically shield the metal-ligand bonds. Recently, we demonstrated the efficacy of this approach by adopting a linker with tridentate nature and having Ni(II) as the metal center (Fig. 8a) [162]. The utilization of higher dentate linkers has been known to enhance stability [145] owing to affordance of kinetic stability, but such a study was restricted to charged N-donor based MOFs. The cationic MOF viz. IPM-MOF-201 was not only found to be stable in water but also over a wide pH range of 4 to $1\ N\ N{\mathsf{a O H}}$ . The higher concentration of organic groups around the metal center improved local hydrophobicity and subsequently stabilized the MOF. Wang and co-workers further reported the utilization of this approach by utilizing a neutral hydrophobic ligand [tetrakis[4-(1- imidazolyl)phenyl]methane] with higher denticity in a cationic MOF viz. SCU-102 (Fig. 8b) [163]. The authors reported the presence of local hydrophobicity to favorably aid in retention of crystallinity during ion-exchange process.
## 4. Applications and emerging frontiers
MOFs fabricated from N-donor linkers offer several distinct advantages, in addition to the features carried by the crystalline porous coordination networks such as control over architectures, tunable pore chemistry. Although these compounds were proposed to be effective for applications in different domains, the research was largely focused on structural aspects and design approaches and their role as co-linkers. More recently, with the advent of the field, MOFs constructed out of such linkers too have found suitability for applications in a wide span such as molecular sieving, gas/solvent storage, magnetism, catalysis, capture of priority pollutants (Scheme 4). Apart from these conventional applications, such MOFs have been found to be suitable for recent emerging applications such as formation of glass, water splitting etc. To provide the reader a flavor of different possible applications, a recent survey of the various applications based on N-donor linker based MOFs are described in the following sub-sections.
## 4.1. Gas storage and separation
One of the major applications using MOFs as sorbents is that of molecular storage and sieving which has been highly sought after over the last couple of decades. The ability of MOFs to adsorb gases has spanned across a wide array of important molecules including $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , CO, $S0_{2}$ , NO, hydrocarbons, inert gases etc. In particular the feasibility of having modular and tunable architectures combined with specific interacting sites has led to MOFs scoring significantly above congener porous materials. It is worthwhile to mention that initial reports of gas adsorption using porous coordination polymers was reported for systems constructed from neutral N-donor linkers [10], where the authors reported porous nature under high pressure. Thereafter several compounds for varied applications have emerged. The access to modulate structural features for specific applications has been demonstrated in N-donor linker based compounds. Such modulation of the linker length, metal ion and inorganic anions can render frameworks with interesting facets. For instance, shorter linkers such as pyrazine give rise to ultramicroporous networks (pore diameter $<7\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ which have higher electronic density overlap thus enhancing the performance for the capture of polar gases such as ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ in low pressure region as compared to linkers with higher length. Also, the bridging anions present in the framework viz. $\mathsf{S I F}_{6}^{2-}$ , $\mathrm{TiF}_{6}^{2-}$ , $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ etc. have been found to affect the capture performance via unique binding sites and tailoring pore chemistry. The choice metal ion also plays a very crucial role as they determine the metal-nitrogen bond distance which affects the overall pore diameter hence displaying unique properties as compared to frameworks built from other transition elements [170]. Some of the key results for MOFs based on Ndonor linkers for gas adsorption applications have been discussed in the following section.
## 4.1.1. Carbondioxide $(C O_{2})$
For neutral ligand based MOFs, the choice anions invariably bestows functional sites to the pore channels, which can be utilized for different applications. Several such compounds have been studied in the literature (Table 1), among which some of the representative examples have been discussed below.
In this regard Zaworotko and co-workers reported a series of MOFs based on pyridyl-terminal linkers and connected by inorganic anions such as $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}$ and $\mathsf{C r O}_{4}^{2-}$ viz. $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{b p e})_{2}\mathsf{M o O}_{4}]$ MOOFOUR-1-Co, $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{b p e})_{2}\mathsf{C r O}_{4}]$ CROFOUR-1-Co, $[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{b p e})_{2}\mathsf{M o O}_{4}]$ MOOFOUR-1-Ni and $[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{b p e})_{2}\mathsf{C r O}_{4}]$ CROFOUR-1-Ni; where bpe represents 1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)ethane (Fig. 9a) [164]. Owing to the presence of polar groups aligned along the pore channel, the compounds exhibited high loading of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at low pressure (Fig. 9b) and high heat of adsorptions (56 and $50\ \mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}$ for MOOFOUR-1-Ni and CROFOUR-1-Ni respectively), which are comparable to values attained by MOFs having open metal sites.
Among other aspects, the character of the pore surface has been found to play a crucial role in bestowing selectivity for gas adsorption. In this regard, Navarro and co-workers reported exploration of gas adsorption properties of isoreticular series of MOFs by varying the secondary functional groups from $-\mathsf{H}$ to $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ , $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ , –OH and $-S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ [165]. The authors employed a bidentate pyrazolate based linker viz. $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D P}$ [1,4-bis(1H-pyrazol-4-yl)benzene] and studied this behavior in two kinds of MOFs viz. NiBDP and ZnBDP. Detailed characterization and gas adsorption studies were carried out targeting relevant gas mixtures $(\mathsf{N}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , ${C O_{2}}/{C_{2}}/{\mathrm{H}_{2}})$ . Despite significant variation in the secondary functional sites, gas separation efficiency was not found to be improved significantly for polar gas mixture $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{2})$ . But in the case of other two mixtures, the role of polar sites was substantiated, with notable improvement for selectivity in adsorption as observed from partition coefficients. The authors attributed the pronounced effect of favorable interactions with polar molecules over apolar gas adsorptive. In another work of exploring the role of secondary polar sites, Zhang and co-workers explored the role of hydroxide as active sites for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption [166]. Hydroxide functionalized isostructural MOFs of the previously reported compounds viz. MAF-X25 $\left[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}(\mathrm{bbta})\right]$ and MAF-X27 $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{b b t a})]$ (where $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{t}\mathsf{a}=1H,5H$ -benzo(1,2-d:4,5-d’)bistriazole) were synthesized and all the four compounds were studied in detail for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption. The two new compounds viz. MAF-X25ox $\mathrm{\Delta[Mn^{II}M n^{I I I}(O H)C l_{2}(b b t a)]}$ and MAF-X27ox $[\mathsf{C o}^{\mathrm{II}}\mathsf{C o}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathsf{O H})\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{b b t a})]$ were thoroughly characterized and were found to show different coordination environments owing to presence of additional coordinating group. The hydroxide functionalized MOFs were found to present significantly higher uptakes over non-functionalized ones, with high slopes at low pressure and the gravimetric uptake capacities (7.1 and $6.7\mathrm{\mmolg}^{-1}$ for MAF-X25ox and MAF-X27ox respectively) were comparable to the MOFs registering highest uptakes. As –OH groups are prone to reaction with ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to form bicarbonates, the authors evaluated the sorption features of the compounds even further. As anticipated the compounds exhibited very high heats of adsorption (120 and $124~\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}$ for MAF-X25ox and MAF-X27ox respectively), which could be correlated to the chemisorptions interaction. Temperature swing adsorption suggested that the desorption is relatively fast considering the very strong affinity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . The selectivity for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ was further evaluated using breakthrough experiments.
More recently, Rossin, Galli and co-workers reported the effect of nitro $\left(-\mathsf{N O}_{2}\right)$ pendant groups in a series of MOFs [167]. 3 compounds viz. $\mathsf{M}(\mathsf{B P Z N O}_{2};$ (M - Co, Cu, Zn and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPZNO}_{2}\ensuremath{\mathrm{~-~}}3$ -nitro$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyrazole) were synthesized and screened for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption properties. The MOFs had a 3D structure with square pore channels in case of Co and Zn-MOFs, while Cu showed aligned ${\bf-N O}_{2}$ groups in rhombic porous channels. Under ambient conditions (1.2 bar and 298 K), the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF had an uptake of $21.8\%$ $(4.95\mathrm{mmol/g})$ for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ with a Henry selectivity $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2})$ of 15 and IAST factor of 12 at pressure of 1 bar and $298\mathrm{K}.$ Interestingly, the authors observed that the interaction site in the compounds was between the O-atoms of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and N-atoms of the $-\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ groups.
To study the effect of pore chemistry towards ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption in greater depth, Zaworotko and co-workers provided a seminal contribution, wherein the detailed adsorption study of variants of SIFSIX-1-Cu with differential pore sizes was reported [168]. The authors reported synthesis of SIFSIX-2-Cu $[\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{d p a})_{2}(\mathsf{S i F}_{6})]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (dpa $\it{\Delta}-\ 4,4^{\prime}$ -dipyridylacetylene) and an interpenetrated analogue viz. SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, with individual nets similar to SIFSIX-2-Cu (Fig. 10a). Additionally, SIFSIX-3-Zn $[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{pyr})_{2}(\mathrm{SiF}_{6})]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (pyr - pyrazine), was also employed for comparing the performance. The authors carried out single-component gas adsorption studies and breakthrough analysis to understand the adsorption tendencies of the compounds (Fig. 10b, 10c). The interpenetrated version SIFSIX-2-Cu-i exhibited very high gravimetric uptake of $238~\mathrm{mg/g}$ ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar. In case of SIFSIX-3-Zn, very high selectivity at low partial pressure was observed, which can comparable to amino-pendant MOFs or mesoporous silica bearing primary amine sites. Also the authors found that this compound displayed very fast kinetics for the uptake of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over other gases such as $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , $0_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , which is a key finding in terms of developing sorbents which exhibit selectivity in terms of both thermodynamics and kinetics. Using a high-throughput multicomponent adsorption set-up it was reported that the adsorption was being affected by water [169]. Later, Eddaoudi and co-workers explored another facet of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ capture using MOFs, by understanding the energetic of adsorption with regard to pore size of microporous MOFs having similar pore surfaces [170]. The authors synthesized a microporous MOF viz. SIFSIX-3-Cu $\mathsf{I C u}(\mathsf{p y r})_{2}(\mathsf{S i F}_{6})\bigr]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (ligand - pyrazine and $\mathsf{S i F}_{6}^{2-}$ as anion), and compared the adsorption performance with previously reported compounds viz. SIFSIX-3-Zn $\left[{\mathrm{Zn}}({\mathrm{pyr}})_{2}({\mathrm{SiF}}_{6})\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ and SIFSIX-2-Cu-i (ligand - dipyridylacetylene). The $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ -centered MOF exhibited steeper ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption accompanied with higher heats of adsorption, over the isostructural $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -based MOF, which the authors attributed to the contraction of the pore owing to JahnTeller distortion. Interestingly the decrease in pore size from SIFSIX-2-Cu-i $(5.15\mathring\mathrm{A})$ to SIFSIX-3-Zn (3.84 Å) has found to have a substantial effect on the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake amounts.
The same group later explored another kind of fluorous MOF viz. NbOFFIVE-1-Ni $[\mathrm{NiNbOF}_{5}(\mathrm{pyrazine})_{2}]$ for its ability to adsorb ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ (Fig. 11) [171]. The MOF comprised of square porous channels having a diameter of 3.210(8) Å. The MOF was found to present high thermal and water stability and also resistance upon $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ exposure. The authors observed $340\%$ enhancement in volumetric uptake at 400 ppm of NbOFFIVE-1-Ni over SIFSIX-3-Ni in case of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , which was ascribed to the stronger interactions with the pore wall due to contracted pores (Fig. 11). The authors were successful in ascribing precise host–guest interactions by collecting SCXRD data of the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorbed crystal. The position of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ was found to be highly favorable as the C-atom in ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ had a strong interaction with 4 neighboring fluorine atoms. Mixed gas breakthrough experiments and adsorption in dry and humid phases were also carried out to test and validate the potential of the compound for real-time scenario.
The effect of pore surface in isostructural MOFs was further explored by Zaworotko and co-workers by comparing the performance of TIFSIX-3-Ni with SIFSIX-3-Ni and other benchmark MOF compounds [172]. As anticipated from previous literature knowledge [173], the shift from $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ to $\mathrm{TiF}_{6}^{2-}$ resulted in favorable interactions and higher $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ selectivity. TIFSIX-3-Ni reported very high adsorption in terms of uptake $(17.1\%)$ and the stability was found to be superior to the reported analogue SIFSIX-3-Ni. Bu, Feng and co-workers also contributed to the development of $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ based MOFs towards ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption [174]. Two MOFs based on porphyrin backbone were synthesized viz. CPM-131 (Fe) and CPM132 (Zn) based on TPyP linker (tetra(4-pyridyl)metalloporphryin) having structural features similar to the well-studied SIFSIX series MOFs. Both the compounds exhibited high ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptakes and relatively lower $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ uptakes. CPM-131 exhibited an uptake for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ of $101\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ at $273\mathrm{K}$ and 1 atm, whereas CPM-132 had an uptake of $74\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ under the same conditions. IAST calculations revealed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (50:50) selectivity of 108 in case of CPM-131 and 104 for CPM-132. The authors attributed the higher uptakes and selectivity to the presence of favorable electrostatics.
Navarro and co-workers extended their work on pyrazolatebased MOFs to report a rare V(IV)-centered framework [175]. The
MOF $[\mathsf{V}_{3}(\mathsf{\textmu_{3}}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O})0(\mathsf{O H})_{2}(\mathsf{\textmu_{4}}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{B P D})_{1.5}(\mathsf{\textmu_{-}H C O O})_{3}]$ , where BDP represents benzene-1,4-bipyrazolate, comprised of a triangular trinuclear vanadium(IV) SBU (secondary building unit) wherein the vanadium nodes have distorted octahedral geometries. The structure exhibited high porosity but more interestingly displayed interparticle mesoporosity as revealed by low temperature $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ (77 K) gas adsorption isotherm. Owing to the presence of several polar groups aligned along the pore walls, the authors carried out detailed adsorption studies to understand the selectivity of the MOF towards $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ mixtures. In addition, owing to the mesopores the MOF was also tested for the feasibility of separating VOC mixtures such as benzene and cyclohexane. In another report of employing pyrazolate linkers, Xie, Li and co-workers investigated ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption in a series of functionalized MOFs viz. $[\mathrm{Ni}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{BDP-X})_{6}]$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDP}-1,4$ -bis(4-pyrazolyl)benzene, X - CHO, CN, COOH) (Fig. 12) [176]. Owing to the presence of Ni (II) metal nodes and strongly coordinating pyrazolate terminal groups, the MOF was found to present exceptional stability under high basic conditions. The authors carried out IAST calculations to ascertain the selectivity for the 3 MOFs. The $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity was found to be in the range of 21.1–23.7, 18.3–19.1 and $30.6-$ 38.5 for –CHO, –CN and –COOH functionalized MOFs respectively (Fig. 12). The corresponding $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ selectivity were in the range of 4.4–5.1, 4.6–5.8, 6.6–9.5. In few other reports too, N-donor linker based MOFs have been shown to be effective sorbents for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ .
For instance, Brooker, Kepert and co-workers reported the synthesis of a $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ -based MOF viz. $\{[\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{p y}\mathsf{-p z p y p z})_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C N})_{2}]$  $\mathsf{M e C N}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (py-pzpypz  4-(4-pyridyl)-2,5-dipyrazylpyridine) which had an unusual topology and comprised of zigzag shaped channels [177]. The MOF was found to present a selectivity of 4.8:1 for a mixed-gas condition $(\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}~15{:}85)$ at 1 bar, with a moderate heat of adsorption of $32~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ .
Zaworotko and co-workers extended their work using fluorinerich anions by investigating the separation for CO and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ [178]. From their extensive studies, two compounds viz. SIFSIX-3-Ni and TIFSIX-2-Cu-i were evaluated for their adsorption tendencies for the described gas mixture. SIFSIX-3-Ni exhibited a selectivity of $>4000$ for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C O}$ mixtures (Fig. 13). The authors ascribed the high affinity for the microporous fluorine-rich porous compounds towards ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ for the observed selectivity. Owing to the observed selectivity, the authors obtained pure CO feed $(99.99\%)$ for mixtures containing $1\%$ and $50\%$ ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ by adsorption.
Long and co-workers reported the aspect of flexibility for the adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The authors employed a ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -based MOF viz. Co(bdp) where $\mathsf{b d p}^{2-}$ stands for 1,4- benzenedipyrazolate (Fig. 14a) [179]. The framework exhibited very unusual gate opening adsorption patterns for different gases such as ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ under the same temperature (Fig. 14b). The authors attributed this behavior to the stabilization provided by the adsorbate to the open phase of the compound. Mixed-gas studies were also carried out using the MOF for different mixtures of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ to understand the structural flexibility under different conditions. In a similar manner, Eddaoudi and co-workers screened a series of MOFs constructed from inorganic anions as pillars, for their adsorption tendency towards ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ [180]. Both ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ are impurities in the raw natural gas and hence its concurrent removal is an important application. The authors obtained differential responses with SIFSIX-2-Ni-i having $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}/\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ selectivity>1, while SIFSIX-3-Ni had it $\mathbf{\Psi}<1$ . Interestingly AlFFIVE1-Ni displayed a selectivity of approximately 1. Binary mixture studies were also reported in addition to examination of singlecomponent adsorption isotherms.
## 4.1.2. Hydrocarbons
Petroleum industry requires separation of different hydrocarbon mixtures to obtain pure feeds. By virtue of having tailored functionalities and tunable architectures, MOFs have been found to be useful to separate both gaseous and liquid hydrocarbons [181,182]. In this regard, N-donor linker based MOFs too have been found to present utility for certain applications (Table 2). Some of the key results in this regard are discussed below.
One mixture which requires attention is the separation of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{6}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ . Among the different strategies of using MOFs as sorbents for separations is the utilization of open metal sites, where unsaturated hydrocarbons can interact favorably. Also ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{6}$ is less polar than ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ , which has yielded the employment of polar secondary sites as a pathway to obtain selectivity. Yet the separation of ethane from ethylene is further required as obtaining pure feed of ethylene is challenging. To demonstrate the efficacy of MOFs for such applications, Zhang and co-workers reported the synthesis and exploration of MAF-49 $\mathrm{[Zn(batz)]}.0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $\mathrm{\textmu}_{2}\mathrm{bat}z$ - bis(5-ami no-1H-1,2,4-triazol-3-yl)methane) towards hydrocarbon adsorption [183]. The MOF was found to exhibit high hydrolytic and chemical resistance. The authors carried out single component adsorption for the different gases $(\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{6},\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf H_{4},\mathsf C0_{2},\mathsf C\mathsf H_{4})$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ , 307 K and 316 K, where the guest binding affinity was found to followed the order of $\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{6}>\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}>\mathrm{CO}_{2}>\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (Fig. 15a, 15b). IAST was used to predict the selectivity of gas mixtures. The selectivity for the MOF for the following mixtures $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C O_{2}}$ , $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C H_{4}}$ at $100\mathrm{kPa}$ and $316\mathrm{K}$ were estimated to be 9, 40, 170 respectively. Notably in the domain of MOFs the selectivity for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ was much higher than the previous reported value (IRMOF-8 with a value of 2.4 at 318 K). Although the present MOF has several polar sites, the selectivity for the non-polar molecule was found to be higher than competing polar molecules. The authors carried out Monte Carlo simulation to elucidate the underlying reason, which suggested that the binding energies were remarkably different for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}\:(\mathrm{-}56.7\mathrm{kJmol^{-1}}.$ ), $\mathsf{C}_{2}\mathsf{H}_{4}(-45.5\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1})$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}(-41.3\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}.$ ) and the optimized structures suggested strong hydrogen bonding multiple interaction sites in case of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{6}$ (Fig. 15c-15 h).
Likewise, obtaining pure feed of acetylene is highly desired, as this gas is essential for several applications. A major impurity in this stream is that of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . Zaworotko and co-workers evaluated two MOFs having similar pore sizes and chemistry viz. SIFSIX-3- Ni (ligand - pyrazine and $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ as pillar) and TIFSIX-2-Cu-i (ligand  1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)acetylene and $\mathrm{TiF}_{6}^{2-}$ as pillar), for the separation of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ [173]. SIFSIX-3-Ni displayed higher uptake for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ at $298\mathrm{K}$ in the low pressure region $_{(<0.3}$ bar), which suggested higher affinity of the compound towards ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ . TIFSIX-2-Cu-i had different uptake patterns, with higher loading for ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ over ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at lower pressure. The authors attributed this behavior to the effect of molecular trap in case of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ . Selectivity studies revealed a $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C O_{2}}$ selectivity of 6.5 at 298 K and $100\mathrm{kPa}$ for TIFSIX-2-Cu-i while the selectivity of ${\ C O_{2}}/{C_{2}}/{\ C_{2}}/{\ C_{2}}$ for SIFSIX-3-Ni was found to be 7.7. Importantly, SIFSIX-3-Ni was found to be a rare example showing selectivity of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ . The authors also verified the humidity stability of the compounds, where both the MOFs showed satisfactory resistance over a prolonged period.
Another important facet of obtaining pure acetylene is the separation of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ . Owing to potential of SIFSIX-based compounds, the separation of these hydrocarbons was investigated [184]. Thorough single component and multi-component studies were carried out to understand the effect of pore size and pore surface characteristics on the binding affinity towards acetylene and the separation ability. IAST calculations revealed that SIFSIX-2- Cu-i registered very high $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ selectivity of 39.7 to 44.8, while SIFSIX-1-Cu set a benchmark for the mass separation under ambient conditions. The authors attribute the presence of sweet spot for favorable interactions with the pore walls for effective separation. Further, another SIFSIX variant was explored for the above-described separation [185]. A longer linker viz. $^{4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-}}$ azopyridine was used to prepare an analogous compound viz. SIFSIX-14-Cu-i (UTSA-200) $\mathsf{I C u}(\mathsf{a z p y})_{2}(\mathsf{S i F}_{6})\mathsf{I}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (Fig. 16). Single component adsorption for $C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ resulted in an uptake of $116\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{K}$ . Although the uptake amounts for acetylene are similar to some previously reported compounds, it exhibits lowest uptake for ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ among these materials. The selectivity was reported using IAST for a binary mixture (1:99 for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4})}$ which resulted in a value of 6000 at 1 bar and $298\mathrm{K},$ which resulted in superior production of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ with a high purity of $99.9999\%$ . Zaworotko and co-workers further explored this class of compounds by investigating ${\sf C}_{2}$ -hydrocarbon separation using a new variant viz. TIFSIX-4-Cu-i (ligand 1,4-bis(4-pyridyl) benzene), which had an interpenetrated structure [186]. The compound upon desolvation underwent structural transformation to an offset form. The authors carried out single-component and breakthrough adsorption experiments to understand the selectivity patterns in previously reported MOFs viz. TIFSIX-2-Cu-i and SIFSIX-2-Cu-i to understand the role of both pore chemistry and effect of the inorganic pillar. The adsorption studies suggested that the longer ligand led to weaker affinity towards $C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and the substitution of $\mathrm{TiF}_{6}^{2-}$ with $\mathrm{SiF}_{6}^{2-}$ led to substantial improvement in the uptake for ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ . In an interesting report, Zaworotko and co-workers controlled the extent of interpenetration in microporous MOFs and studied the consequent effect in the separation of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}$ -hydrocarbons [187].The hypothesis was based on the tradeoff between higher selectivity and working capacity of the MOFs. The authors employed SIFSIX-14-Cu-i and obtained different compounds with variable extent of interpenetration $(99\%,93\%$ , $89\%$ , $70\%$ . The binary breakthrough experiments for $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ mixtures (1:99) yielded that as the extent of interpenetration decreases, the separating ability of the compounds decrease.
Xing and co-workers also explored the fluorine rich microporous MOFs (SIFSIX analogues and NbOFFIVE-series MOFs) for hydrocarbon separation applications (Fig. 17) [188]. Unlike separation of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}$ -hydrocarbons, the authors focused on capture or segregation of ${{C}_{3}}$ -hydrocarbons, specifically the mixture of $\mathrm{C_{3}H_{6}/C_{3}H_{4}}$ . Among the studied compounds, SIFISX-3-Ni and SIFSIX-2-Cu-i had a separation capacity for the removal of ${\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ from ${C_{3}}\mathrm{{H}}_{6}$ of $1187\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{g}$ and $1089\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{g}$ respectively. The authors proposed that the compounds function as a molecular trap which was validated by modeling studies. The same group extended the work in few related compounds, wherein the central atom of the anion was replaced with Ti and Ge to obtain new compounds viz.
TIFSIX-14-Cu-i and GeFSIX-14-Cu-i [189]. The two compounds were tested for their ability to separate ${\sf C}_{3}$ -hydrocarbons. The authors reported very high selectivity for $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{4}$ over ${C_{3}}\mathrm{{H}}_{6}$ which was ascribed to H-bonding interactions with the inorganic pillars and organic backbone, as understood from DFT calculations. Eddaoudi and co-workers also demonstrated the sieving of ${\sf C}_{3}$ - hydrocarbons in a MOF having pore channels decorated with fluorous groups [190]. The authors investigated this separation using NbOFFIVE-1-Ni (KAUST-7), which comprises of pyrazine linkers. The MOF was found to be non-porous to low-temperature $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption, but the micorporosity was confirmed by ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption at room temperature. The compound was found to present high resistance upon exposure to water vapor, $\mathsf{H}_{2}S$ , suggesting the high stability of the MOF. Upon performing singlecomponent gas adsorption experiments, the authors found that the compound permeated entry to ${\mathsf{C}}_{3}{\mathsf{H}}_{6}$ but no adsorption was found for ${\sf C}_{3}{\sf H}_{8}$ . The authors ascribed the selectivity to the pore size and shape to permit the entry of ${C_{3}}\mathrm{{H}}_{6}$ selectively. Detailed mixedcomponent breakthrough measurements were also carried out in this work to validate the superior selectivity.
The aspect of flexibility for the adsorption of gas molecules was reinstated by Zhang and co-workers [191]. The authors chose 3 microporous compounds viz. $[Z\mathrm{n}_{3}(\mathsf{v t}z)_{6}]$ (MAF-123-Zn), $[\mathrm{Mn}_{3}(\mathrm{vtz})_{6}]$ (MAF-123-Mn) and $[\mathsf{C d}_{3}(\mathsf{v t z})_{6}]$ (MAF-123-Cd) (where Hvtz 1,2,3-triazole), to test the adsorption behaviors. Low temperature $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption revealed differential uptake patterns, with Zn-compound showing type-I isotherm, while Mn and Cd had stepwise adsorption, which suggested flexibility of the framework. The authors used SCXRD technique to understand the effect of $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption on the structure, which divulged remarkable distortion to the structure in case of Cd-MOF. To tap the flexibility and presence of polar sites in the compound, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ adsorption measurements were recorded. While Cd and Mn displayed one step adsorption for ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ , Zn-compound exhibited multi-step adsorption. The uptake studies suggested that ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ could insert into the pockets of the compounds and such access was governed by the radii of the central metal node. Likewise, Zhang and co-workers recently explored a series of MOFs having different functional character for the separation of $C_{4}$ -hydrocarbons [192]. In this work, the authors found that compound $[Z\mathsf{n}(\mathsf{b}\mathsf{t m})_{2}]$ $:{\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{b}}\tan{\ }$ - bis(5-methyl-1H-1,2,4-triazol-3-yl) exhibited guest responsive pore conformational changes, which were effective for the purification of 1,3-butadiene. MOFs in general have the attribute of being able to elucidate host–guest interactions via crystallographic evidence. Retrieval of such information is key to develop novel materials for specific applications. In this regard, Dong and co-workers utilized a Cd(II)-based MOF viz. $\mathrm{[Cd(L)_{2}(C l O_{4})]H_{2}O}$ , where L  4-amino-3,5-bis(4-pyridyl-3-phenyl)-1,2,4-triazole, and exposed the desolvated phase to ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ [193]. The authors could obtain the crystallographic data for the guest entrapped phase (Fig. 18). The framework was found to be similar to the host compound with inclusion of acetylene molecules. The ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ molecules were found to have strong interactions with $\mathsf{C l O}_{4}^{-}$ anions. As an additional proof, the authors recorded $\mathrm{^{1}H N M R}$ for the guest encapsulated phase, which validated the inclusion. The reversibility of the adsorption process was monitored using NMR spectral studies. A similar set of experiments was carried out using ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{4}$ , wherein only minor loading for the guest could be confirmed in the $\mathrm{^1H}$ NMR spectra. The distinction in the observed studies was attributed to the acidity of the two guest molecules to interact with the basic perchlorate anions. As a control, the authors synthesized another MOF bearing $\mathsf{B F}_{4}^{-}$ anions, which exhibited similar interactions, as confirmed by crystallographic studies. Detailed studies for binary mixtures $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{2}/C_{2}H_{4}}$ were carried out wherein the compound bearing perchlorate anions was found to be a better separating sorbent.
## 4.1.3. Other gases
Another mixture of challenge for industries is the separation of xenon and krypton. The state-of-art processes utilize cryogenic distillation which is energy intensive and hence MOFs offer an alternate approach which is based on physisorption based separation. Thallapally and co-workers reported the investigation of 2 ultra-microporous MOFs viz. CROFOUR-1-Ni $[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{b p e})_{2}\mathsf{C r O}_{4}]$ (bpe 1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)ethane) and CROFOUR-2-Ni $[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{a z p})_{2}\mathsf{C r O}_{4}]$ $\mathsf{a z p}-4,4^{\prime}$ -azopyridine) towards the adsorption of Xe and Kr (Fig. 19) [194]. The compounds had two kinds of pore surfaces, one lined with O-atoms from the inorganic pillars and the other from the central portion of the organic linkers. The authors recorded adsorption isotherms for Xe and Kr at $298\mathrm{K}$ ， $288\mathrm{K}$ and $278\mathrm{K}.$ The uptake amounts at $298\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar for Xe were found to be $39.6~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for CROFOUR-1-Ni and $36~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for CROFOUR-2-Ni, while the corresponding amounts for Kr were found to be $11~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $11.5\:\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ respectively. IAST calculations revealed Xe/Kr (50:50) selectivity of 26 and 16 for CROFOUR1-Ni and CROFOUR-2-Ni respectively, while the corresponding selectivity was 22 and 15.5 respectively for 20:80Xe/Kr mixture. These values are comparable to benchmark materials reported in the literature. Molecular simulations suggested that both Xe and Kr had similar adsorption sites, wherein O-atoms from the pillars interact. The favorable interactions along with synergistic matching of kinetic diameter with the pore size led to favorable adsorption of Xe, as Kr has lesser polarizability.
Thallapally, Zaworotko and co-workers further explored the ability of MOFs constructed from inorganic anions as pillars towards capture of Xe and Kr [195]. The authors synthesized a new analogue of the SIFSIX series viz. SIFSIX-3-Fe which is isostructural with SIFSIX-3-Ni. Adsorption isotherms were recorded for the two compounds at different temperatures, wherein Ni-MOF exhibited drastically different uptake pattern with an inflection point, but the Fe-MOF had a smooth uptake profile. The adsorption isotherm for Kr, displayed to such inflection point although the gases have similar sizes. This observation was explained by the rotation of the rings which results in an inflection point. The configuration of the linkers in the square shaped pore was supported by theoretical analysis.
Ammonia is one of the major industrial gases which are produced on a large scale. It is highly corrosive and toxic and hence its remediation from air and water streams is of high relevance and importance. Commercialized sorbents have limitations of low capacities and affinities. Owing to ability to tune chemical and physical properties, MOFs can function as efficient sorbents to capture such species. In this regard, Dinca and co-workers reported a series of mesoporous MOFs viz. $\mathrm{M_{2}C l_{2}(B T D D)(H_{2}O)_{2}}$ $_{\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}B T D D}}$ - bis (1H-1,2,3-triazolo[4,5-b], $[4^{\prime},5^{\prime}-\mathrm{i}]$ )dibenzo[1,4]dioxin; M – Co, Mn, Ni) based on triazolate linkers and investigated the ammonia sorption ability (Fig. 20) [196]. The MOFs have a honeycomb network have a porous channel along the $c$ -axis with mesopores of $22{\mathring{\mathsf{A}}}.$ . The coordinated solvent molecules were replaced with methanol and then desolvated by heating under vacuum to yield unsaturated metal sites ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ adsorption isotherms were recorded for all the activated phases of the 3 compounds. The isotherms displayed a sharp uptake at low pressure region with uptake capacities of $15.47\ \mathrm{mmol/g},\ 12\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ and $12.02\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}$ for Mn, Co and Ni analogues. As a control experiment, the authors recorded ammonia sorption for $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ , which showed an uptake of
$9.84\mathrm{mmol/g}$ . Although the capacities were similarly high, $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ was found to $10s e>50\%$ of the capacity after 3 cycles of adsorption, while all the other 3 MOFs reported in this work were found to retain capacity. The authors attributed the use of azolate linkers as the primary reason for the observed stability of the compounds.
The authors extended work by understanding the adsorption of ammonia in similar triazolate based MOFs with smaller pore sizes. The MOFs viz. $\mathtt{M}_{2}\mathtt{C l}_{2}\mathtt{B B T A}$ (M - Co, Ni, Cu and BBTA 1H,5H-benzo (1,2-d),(4,5-d’)bistriazole), having similar pore character and the presence of open metal sites were evaluated for the function of ammonia adsorption [197]. The uptake amounts were found to be $17.95\mathrm{mmol/g}$ , $14.68\mathrm{mmol/g}$ and $19.79\mathrm{mmol/g}$ for Co, Ni and Cu-MOFs respectively. The enhanced uptake over larger analogues was attributed to the higher density of ammonia permitting hydrogen-bond interactions with adjacent guest molecules. This hypothesis was further validated by the observation that Cu-MOF became amorphous upon exposure to 1 bar of ${\mathsf{N H}}_{3}$ , while Co
MOF resisted structural integrity upto 0.1 bar only. The Ni-MOF was found to be stable and detailed analysis were reported for the effect of pore size on the adsorption patterns of ammonia.
The same group also worked on investigating azolate-based systems for the selective binding of $0_{2}$ (Fig. 21) [200]. A cobalt analogue of above described compound viz. Co-BTTri was synthesized which exhibited strong adsorption affinity towards $0_{2}$ over $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ in the desolvated phase, which was directly reflected in the heat of adsorption values $\left(-34\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol}\right.$ for $0_{2}$ v/s $-12\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ for $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ ). Remarkably, such strong affinity is not usually possible for carboxylate-based linkers as the ${\sf C o}({\sf I I})$ centers are electronically poor. The authors could understand the binding tendencies via SCXRD studies which revealed that $0_{2}$ coordinated end-on to every vacant Co-site. Electronic structural calculations suggested that the binding involved partial electron transfer. In pursuit of further exploration, the authors replaced the linker with one having higher electron donating tendency viz. $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BDTriP}-5,5^{\prime}.$ -(5-(1H-pyrazol-4- yl)-1,3-phenylene)bis(1H-1,2,3-triazole)), which led to formation of an isostructural framework. The latter compound had significant affinity towards $0_{2}$ $(\mathrm{Q}_{s t}=-47\mathrm{kJ/mol})_{.}$ . The authors found that the Co-BTTri involves formation of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ -dioxygen species while in case of Co-BDTriP, Co(III)-superoxo complexes are formed.
Removal of $S0_{2}$ has found great relevance towards the purification of natural gas. MOFs have been seldom proposed for the capture and selective sieving of $S0_{2}$ . In this regard, the potential of mircorporous MOFs composed of inorganic pillars was tested in SIFSIX-based compounds [201]. Remarkably high uptake capacity of $11.01\mathrm{mmol/g}$ for $S0_{2}$ was reported in case of SIFSIX-1-Cu, while SIFSIX-2-Cu-i exhibited unusually high adsorption at low pressure $(4.16\mathrm{mmol/g}$ and $2.31\mathrm{mmol}/\mathrm{g}$ at 0.01 bar and 0.002 bar respectively). The observed uptakes were rationalized by interaction between the pore walls and the $S0_{2}$ molecules with secondary guest–guest dipolar interactions. IAST calculations disclosed the separation efficiency with selectivity of 86–89 for SIFSIX-2-Cu-i in case of $S0_{2}/C0_{2}$ , while $S O_{2}/N_{2}$ selectivity was reported to be 2510–3145 for SIFSIX-1-Cu. Navarro and co-workers reported the use of defect chemistry for the selective sulfur dioxide adsorption. The authors employed previously investigated Ni(II)-pyrazolate based stable MOFs viz. $[\mathrm{Ni_{8}(O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}(B D P-X)_{6}}]$ $\mathrm{^{\prime}H_{2}B D P\ -\ }1,4-$ bis(pyrazol-4-yl)benzene-4-X; X - H, OH, $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ ), to carry out postsynthetic modification for the creation of defects (Fig. 22) [202]. The treatment with potassium hydroxide resulted in formation of defective MOFs viz. $\mathrm{K[Ni_{8}(O H)_{3}(E t O)_{3}(B D P-X)_{5.5}]}$ and $\mathrm{K}_{3}[\mathrm{Ni}_{8}(\mathrm{OH)}_{3}(\mathrm{EtO)}(\mathrm{BDP-O})_{5}]$ , which led to presence of extraframework cations. The compounds were then exchanged with ${\mathsf{B}}{\mathsf{a}}^{2+}$ ions present in the pores. The authors also measured breakthrough curves for a binary mixture of $\Nu_{2}/{\sf S O}_{2}$ (97.5/2.5) at $303\mathrm{K},$ which resulted in moderately high capacities for the MOFs. The authors ascribed the high uptakes for $S0_{2}$ to high basicity of nickel-hydroxide bonds upon formation of defects. Such interactions are known to form ${\mathrm{HSO}}_{3}^{-}$ and $S0_{3}^{2-}$ species. The chemisorption process was validated by DFT simulations.
Very recently, Eddaoudi, Karos and co-workers investigated the gas separation properties by fabricating mixed-matrix membranes using MOFs as fillers [203]. The authors employed 3 MOFs having different fluorinated decoration viz. $[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{N b O F}_{5})(\mathsf{p y z})_{2}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (NbOFFIVE-1-Ni); $[\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{A l F}_{5})(\mathsf{p y z})_{2}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (AlFFIVE-1-Ni) and ${\bigl[}{\mathsf{C u}}({\mathsf{S i F}}_{6})$ $(\mathsf{p y z})_{2}\bigr\rvert_{\mathrm{n}}$ (SIFSIX-3-Cu), wherein pyz stands for pyrazine, and two polymers for their study viz. 6FDA-DAM (6FDA $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(hexafluoroiso propylidene)diphthalic anhydride; DAM 2,4,6-trimethyl-1,3-dia minobenzene] and PDMC (propane-diol monoesterified crosslinked polymer). The authors investigated the gas sorption properties for $\mathsf{H}_{2}S$ , ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ by both single component and mixed-matrix phases.
## 4.2. Pollutant capture
Environmental pollution has a direct impact on the quality of human life and perturbs the ecological balance. The growing clout of pollution across various sources has necessitated the development of novel materials or methods. The trend in the domain of MOFs based on N-donor linkers towards remediation of environmental pollutants has been presented below. Water pollution has become a major global concern, and it continues to command remarkable research attention owing to the growing industrialization and disposal of pollutants across different streams. MOFs have recently emerged as sorbents for the capture of varying types of anions. The ability to tune molecular level functionalities bestows this class of materials superiority over conventionally studied sorbents. MOFs have been found to be suitable for the trapping of different class of pollutants such as heavy-metal oxoanions, radioactive wastes, organic dyes, organic wastes etc. (Table 3). Some of the key recent results based on N-donor linker based MOFs are discussed below.
Oliver and co-workers explored the potential of cationic coordination polymers to entrap permanganate, chromate and perrhenate [204]. The authors employed SLUG-21 $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(4,4^{\prime}-$ $\mathrm{bipy})_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{3}\mathrm{SCH}_{2}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{3})4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}]$ (bipy $^{-4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine) for the aqueous phase ion-exchange based trapping of the above oxoanions. Very high uptake capacities were obtained for permanganate $(292\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ and perrhenate $(602\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ . The same group extended the work of cationic metal-organic materials towards capture applications by preparing a heterometallic coordination polymer viz. $[\mathrm{Zn_{x}C o_{1-x}(H_{2}O)_{4}(4,4^{\prime}-b i p y)_{2}}]^{2+}[205]$ . The compounds showed potential to entrap chromate anions $(68.5\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g})$ even in presence of excess concentration of competing anions such as $\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ and $S0_{4}^{2-}$ . Wang and co-workers reported capture of dichromate in triazole based $\mathsf{A g}(\mathrm{I})$ -centered cationic MOF viz. $\mathrm{\Gamma[Ag_{2}(b t r)_{2}]}.2\mathrm{Cl}0_{4}.3\mathrm{H}_{2}-$ O (btr $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bis(1,2,4-triazole)) [206]. The 3D compound comprised nanoscale cages which held the free anions (Fig. 23). The compound was able to uptake dichromate anions with a capacity of $0.73\mathrm{mol/mol}$ after $48\mathrm{h}$ . The ion-exchange process was validated from SC-SC studies. The authors also tested the selectivity of the capture by investigating uptake trends in binary mixtures. As the MOF was luminescent in nature, the dichromate capture was also monitored in the fluorescence emission profiles. Similar capture of dichromate using $\mathsf{A g}(\mathrm{I})$ and triazole-terminal linker combinations was reported by Ding and co-workers [207] and later by Li and co-workers [208] and Yang and co-workers [209].
Zhang and co-workers reported the dichromate trapping ability of highly porous cationic MOFs [210]. Two $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ -based cationic MOFs were reported viz. FIR-53 $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{tip}{\bf a})_{2}(\mathrm{OH})]{\cdot3\mathrm{NO}_{3}}{\cdot12\mathrm{H}_{2}}0$ and FIR-54 $[\mathrm{Zn(tipa)}]{\cdot2}\mathrm{NO}_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (tipa - tris(4-(1H-imidazol-1-y l)phenyl)amine))) (Fig. 24). The compounds exhibited high uptake capacities for dichromate with $74.2\:\mathrm{mg/g}$ and $103\mathrm{mg/g}$ respectively. The recycling ability of FIR-53 was studied over multiple cycles which yielded no loss of performance or any disintegration to the framework. The dichromate uptake was also characterized using SC-SC studies for FIR-53. Thereafter, our group also reported the applicability of a cationic MOF for the function of capturing oxoanions [211]. By using a rational design strategy, we employed sulfate $(\mathsf{S O}_{4}^{2-}\bar{})$ salt to prepare a water-stable MOF viz. $\{[\mathsf{N i}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{3}(\mathsf{S O}_{4})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]{\cdot}(\mathrm{SO}_{4}){\cdot}\mathrm{xG}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (L - tipa) and use of the anion present in the host framework as a template for selective and rapid capture of similar shaped anions (Fig. 25). The compound was found to be an efficient sorbent for the trapping of dichromate and permanganate, with the latter functioning as a model for pertechnetate anions. The uptake capacity for dichromate was estimated to be $166\mathrm{mg/g}$ . Guo and co-workers reported a cationic MOF constructed from Cd(II) nodes viz. $\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathsf{C d}(\mathrm{tipo})(\mathrm{HCOO})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})]{\cdot}\mathsf{N O}_{3}{\cdot}\mathsf{D M F}$ (tipo - tris(4-(1H-imidzol-1-yl)phosphine oxide) for the function of dichromate capture [212]. The compound bearing two kinds of microporous channels exhibited an uptake capacity of $228~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , with selective uptake even in presence of common ions such as $\mathsf{C l}^{-},\mathsf{B r}^{-},\mathsf{I}^{-},\mathsf{N O}_{3}^{-}$ and $S0_{4}^{2-}$ .
Another class of oxoanions is that of radioactive elements. Prominently among the pollutants is pertechnetate which is a major fission product of Uranium-235 and has long half life and easy diffusion in water. Thus sorbents focusing on the trapping of such ions is highly desired. As $\mathrm{TcO}_{4}^{-}$ is radioactive in nature, researchers have modeled $\mathsf{M n O}_{4}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{R e O}_{4}^{-}$ as anions which show similar physical properties. In this regard, Ziegler and co-workers reported a layered MOF viz. $\mathrm{Pb}[\mathrm{B}(\mathrm{Im})_{4}](\mathrm{NO}_{3})(\mathrm{nH}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ which was tested for the capture of $\mathsf{R e O}_{4}^{-}$ [213]. The authors obtained SC-SC transformation which validated the process, which was supported by $^{207}\mathrm{{Pb}}$ solid-state NMR. Oliver and co-workers examined the ability of another layered material viz. $\mathsf{C u}_{2}(4,4^{\prime}-$ bipy)2 $\mathrm{\Gamma_{1}(O_{3}S C H_{2}C H_{2}S O_{3}){\cdot}3H_{2}}$ (SLUG-22) and earlier studied SLUG21 for the entrapment of $\mathsf{M n O}_{4}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{R e O}_{4}^{-}$ [214]. The authors observed that SLUG-21 was a reversible ion-exchanger but SLUG22 did not show permanganate trapping. The comparative analysis was performed on the two compounds for catalytic activity.
More recently, Wang and co-workers explored a 3D cationic MOF (SCU-100) $\mathrm{\Delta[Ag_{2}(t i p m)]{\cdot}2N O_{3}{\cdot}1.5H_{2}O}$ (tipm - tetrakis[4-(1-im dazolyl)phenyl]methane) to capture $\mathrm{Tc}0_{4}^{-}$ and $\mathsf{R e O}_{4}^{-}$ (Fig. 26) [215]. The compound was found to be robust in nature with resistance over wide pH range (1 to 13) and also unperturbed by $\beta$ and $\gamma$ radiation. The uptake capacity of SCU-100 for $\mathsf{R e O}_{4}^{-}$ was calculated to be $541~\mathrm{mg/g}$ and the corresponding $\mathrm{K_{d}}$ value was $1.9\mathrm{X}10^{5}~\mathrm{mL}/\mathrm{g}$ . The uptake for $\mathrm{Tc}0_{4}^{-}$ was found to be selective even in concurrent presence of interfering anions such as ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-},$ , $S0_{4}^{2-}$ , $C0_{3}^{2-}$ , $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ . To understand the mechanistic details, the authors could obtain the structure after the ion-exchange process; which revealed that $\mathsf{R e O}_{\mp}$  coordinated to the open metal site in the framework. The limited recyclability and lack of stability was overcome in another cationic MOF having mixed linker systems viz. SCU-101 $[\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{tipm})_{2}(\mathsf C_{2}\mathsf O_{4})]$ $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ [216]. The compound was found to have fast capture performance, higher capture amounts and the authors could identify the anion recognition properties. Apart from the general design principles guiding development of cationic MOFs, understanding local environment is a key component. The same group also explored the potential of a coordination polymer SBN viz. [Ag (bipy)] $\left]\left(\mathsf{N O}_{3}\right)$ towards capture of such oxoanions [217]. The compound exhibited remarkably high capture limit of $786\mathrm{mg/g}$ The authors could obtain the structural data for the anion-exchange phase, which aids to probe the underlying mechanism of ion inclusion. Like the previous studies, the authors found that the guest anion coordinated to the metal node. Enthused from the knowledge gained from these studies, the same group more recently reported the capture of $\mathrm{Tc}0_{4}^{-}$ anions by a cationic organic polymer compound [218]. The performance was found to be very effective across different parameters and the material was resistant to radioactive radiation.
Another important pollutant which demands urgent attention is arsenic. Li and co-workers investigated ZIF-8 as a sorbent for the trapping of arsenate, which yield a reasonably high capture limit of $76.5\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g}$ at low concentration $(9.8~\upmu\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{L})$ [219]. Detailed characterization was carried out to understand the mechanism from which the authors inferred that ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ adsorbs on the active sites $(Z\mathrm{n-OH})$ on the external surface of ZIF-8. Zhang and coworkers further investigated such sorption by synthesizing and screening ZIF-8 nanoparticles [220]. The uptake capacities were reported to be $49.49\mathrm{mg/g}$ and $60.03\mathrm{mg/g}$ for $\mathsf{A s}(\mathrm{III})$ and ${\sf A s}({\sf V})$ respectively at $\mathsf{p H}7$ . Interestingly the authors observed that $S0_{4}^{2-}$ and ${\mathsf{N O}}_{3}^{-}$ did not have any significant effect on the adsorption of arsenic, but $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ severely hindered the uptake patterns.
A major class of water pollutants is that of organic pollutants such as dyes, pesticides etc. Du and co-workers reported the capture of a carcinogenic molecule viz. Acid Red 26 via size exclusion effect in a $\mathsf{A g}(\mathrm{I})$ based coordination polymer [221]. The authors prepared the cationic MOF viz. $[\mathsf{A g}_{7}(\mathrm{L})_{6}](\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{7}(\mathsf{s o l v e n t})_{\mathrm{x}}$ (L 3-(2-pyridyl)-4-(3-pyridyl)-5-(4-pyridyl)-1,2,4-triazole) and found crystallographically that exchange with smaller anion such as $\mathsf{B F}_{4}^{-}$ has no effect on the structure. The authors took a set of 13 dyes and explored the dye capture properties of the framework (Fig. 27). Among the different types of dyes chosen, Acid Red 26 had the highest uptake $(96.40\%)$ which was ascribed to the precise size matching with the available pore and charge. The charge selectivity was further ascertained by exchange experiments in binary mixtures of both cationic and anionic dyes. Yang and co-workers comprehensively explored the degradation of dyes in a $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ based MOF viz. $\mathrm{Cu}_{8}(3,5\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Ph}_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{t}z)_{6}(\mathrm{HSO}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{5.5}$ (L: $3,5\mathrm{-Ph}_{2}\mathrm{-tzH}\mathrm{~-~}3,5-$ diphenyl-1,2,4-triazole) [222]. The compound exhibited semiconducting nature and highly efficient substrate for the degradation of several kinds of dye molecules including rhodamine-B, methylene blue, methyl orange, methyl blue. The generation of OH radical in conjunction with $\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{I})/\mathsf{C u}(\mathrm{II})$ redox activity was proposed as the plausible rationale.
Our group recently reported a highly stable cationic framework having bulky counter-ions as a suitable sorbent of organic dyes over a wide pH range (Fig. 28) [162]. A Ni(II)-centered MOF viz. $\{\mathrm{[Ni(L)_{2}]}{\cdot}(\mathrm{BPSA}){\cdot}\mathrm{xG}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (IPM-MOF-201; L - tipa, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BPSA}\ -\ 4,4^{\prime}-$ biphenyldisulfonic acid) had six coordination around the metal node from six independent linkers providing exceptional hydrophobic shielding to the metal ion. This resulted in the compound exhibiting unusual stability over a wide pH range (4 to 14). The presence o the bulky anion in the pores was sought for the trapping of like organic molecules with larger sizes and similar charge. 5 anionic dyes viz. methyl orange (MO), indigo carmine (IC), alizarin red S (ARS), 4-phenylazophenol (PAP) and methyl red (MR) were chosen which had relatively smaller sizes and varying character. Control experiments with a cationic dye viz.
methylene blue (MB) validated the anion-exchange process. The dye capture process was reported over wide pH range, wherein the form of the dye at specific conditions was found to be included in the respective phase of the MOF.
Air pollution has found to have a direct impact on the health of all living beings. Apart from the toxic gases let out by industrial and vehicular pollution, the release of volatile organic compounds (VOCs) from several sources such as paints, personal care products are contributing significantly to the overall contamination of the atmosphere. In this regard, MOFs which can offer a platform for molecular sieving via physisorption have demonstrated promise. Some of the key results have been described below.
Navarro and co-workers proposed the ability of two homoleptic azolate based MOFs viz. Ni(bpb) and $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{b p b})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpb}\ -\ 1,4-(4-$ bispyrazolyl)benzene) to interact with aromatic guest molecules such as benzene [143]. The authors investigated the structural changes accompanying the inclusion of guest molecules, wherein they found that the planar benzene molecule had not a significant impact on the structure, but non-planar DMSO molecule led to larger distortions. Dong and co-workers reported the inclusion of a series of VOCs in a $\mathrm{CdL}_{2}$ (L 4-amino-3,5-bis(4-pyridyl-3-phe nyl-1,2,4-triazole) based microporous MOF in both liquid and gas phases (Fig. 29) [223]. The authors investigated several reactive VOCs such as 2-furaldehyde, 3-furaldehyde, aniline, p-toluidine, m-toluidine, o-toluidine, 2-thenaldehyde, 3-thenaldehyde and found that all the reactive molecules could undergo radical formation under UV-light exposure. The guest inclusion studies were carried out on single crystals of the compound where reactive short lived species were entrapped over a prolonged period of time.
Dong and co-workers explored a $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ -triazole based compound viz. $\{[\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}$ $(\mathrm{L}-4$ -amino-3,5-bis(4-pyridyl-3 -phenyl)-1,2,4-triazole) for the uptake of iodine [224]. The structure comprised of square-shaped porous channels with free anions. The crystals upon iodine exposure were found to undergo color change which was correlated to the inclusion of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ molecules. Similar studies were carried out with different forms of iodine in solution phase well; wherein all the phases were characterized using SC-SC studies. The same group further explored the above compound for the trapping of volatile chlorocarbon viz. ${\mathrm{CH}}_{2}{\mathrm{Cl}}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ and ${\mathsf{C C l}}_{4}$ [225]. The authors could obtain the crystallographic evidence for the guest encapsulated phases, which suggested that the guest molecules were adsorbed via weak H-bond interactions with the framework bearing H-arene and $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ sites. Mixed vapor experiments were carried out to determine the affinity order which was found to be $\mathrm{CHCl_{3}}>\mathrm{CH_{2}C l_{2}}>\mathrm{CCl_{4}}$ . Subsequently another MOF $\{[\mathsf{C u L}_{2}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}]{\cdot}1.75\mathsf{C H}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{C H}_{3}0\mathsf{H}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (L  9-butyl-2,7- di(1H-1,2,4-triazol-1-yl)-9H-carbazole) was reported for the adsorption and separating ability towards BTEX (benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, xylene) and chlorocarbons [226]. Our group recently reported a luminescent MOF having flexible core towards sensing of various VOCs (Fig. 30) [227]. A Cd(II)-based MOF $\{[\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{L})_{2}.$ - $(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{PhNO}_{2}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ was synthesized which underwent structural transformation upon desolvation. Interestingly, when the desolvated phase was exposed to VOCs of varying character such as $\mathrm{Ph-N}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}$ , Ph-CN, Ph- ${\mathsf{O C H}}_{3}$ , benzene, toluene, differential structural responses were obtained. In addition, the luminescence response to each VOC was found to be drastically different both in terms of intensity and peak maxima.
In a seminal contribution, Barea, Navarro and co-workers reported an isoreticular series of hydrophobic MOFs for the trapping of VOCs [157]. The authors chose pyrazolate-terminal linkers to yield series of compounds with formula $\mathrm{\Delta[Ni_{8}(O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}(L)_{6}]_{n}}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{1}-1\mathrm{H}$ -pyrazole-4-carboxylic acid; $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{2}-4$ -(1H-pyrazole-4- yl)benzoic acid; $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{3}\ \mathrm{~-~}\ 4,4^{\prime}$ -benzene-1,4-diylbis(1H-pyrazole); $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{4}\mathrm{~-~}4,4^{\prime}$ -buta-1,3-diyne-1,4-diylbis(1H-pyrazole); $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}_{5}\:-\:4,4^{\prime}-$ (benzene-1,4-diyldiethyne-2,1-diyl)bis(1H-pyrazole); $H_{2}L_{5}–R-R\cdot$ - methyl, trifluoromethyl). Low temperature ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption (77 K) isotherms validated the porous nature of the compounds, which $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adsorption (298 K) confirmed the hydrophobicity. Utilizing the hydrophobic nature of the compounds, the authors tested the ability of the compounds to capture diethylsulfide (DES), which is typically used as a model for mustard gas under both dry and humid conditions. The results were compared to commercially available porous carbon. The results suggested that the uptake of DES was similarly rapid for all the compounds under dry conditions, but varied in presence of moisture. The energetic involved in the process were calculated and correlated to the different functional sites.
## 4.3. Sensing
Sensing is quite relevant to biological systems for small molecule detection, toxic analyte detection thus demanding extensive development of advanced probes. Extensive research towards the fabrication of optical sensors known for their high sensitivity, fast response, selectivity is currently being pursued. Porous materials especially MOFs have shown tremendous scope in this arena due to the widespread availability of linkers thus targeting towards highly selective probe. Nitrogen donor MOFs owing to the flexible nature can incorporate guests and lead to enhanced frameworkanalyte interaction thus maximizing response and sensitivity [228]. Among the different pathways for readout mechanisms,
MOFs offer the platform to design and study luminescent sensors. Although the origin of luminescence in MOFs is attributed to combination of different electronic transitions, the ability to tune electronic properties and pore chemistry renders the development of the non-sophisticated and sensitive method of luminescence read-out a powerful tool to explore. Few of the representative examples exhibiting responsive nature are mentioned below wherein sensing of oxygen, VOCs, anions, industrially relevant gases, toxic analytes etc. have been performed (Table 4).
Broadening the perspective regarding different aromatic analytes, Konar and co-workers have synthesized $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ based framework two dimensional MOF with molecular formula $\mathrm{[Cu(L)(I)]}_{2\mathrm{n}}.2\mathrm{n}$ DMF nMeCN wherein ${\sf L}=4^{\prime}$ -(4-methoxyphenyl)-4,20:60,40’- terpyridine; DMF: dimethylformamide, ${\mathrm{MeCN}}=$ acetonitrile)
[230]. The structural features include two 2D nets forming an interpenetrated framework having a one dimensional channel pore. The authors observed SC-SC transformation for various small molecules viz. DMF:dimethylformamide, $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ :water, DMSO:Dimethylsulfoxide, MeOH: Methanol, Ph-F: Fluorobenzene, Ph-Cl: Chlorobenzene, Ph-Br: Bromobenzene, dea: diethylbenzene, trz: 1,2,4-triazole, py: pyridine, NB: Nitrobenzene, 1,4-DNB: 1,4-nitrobenzene in vapour phase. The authors observed variable supramolecular interactions such as CH- $\pi$ between the small guests molecules and the framework thus stabilizing the small molecules within the framework. A direct influence of the guest exchange was observed on the visible color changes and on the luminescence profile of the compound (Fig. 32). Thus, this MOF can act as a naked eye detector for small molecule. In another work exploring the effect of solvent molecules on the framework, Dong and co-workers have synthesized $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ based MOF viz. $\mathsf{[H}_{2}0\subset\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}\mathsf{I}_{2}$ ; wherein ${\mathrm{L}}=1$ -benzimidazolyl-3, 5-bis(4-pyridyl)benzene] [231]. They utilized this MOF as a probe for detecting small polar aliphatic volatile organic compounds. They probed the interaction site using single crystal X-ray diffraction wherein MeOH, EtOH, n-propanol, $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ were detected and hydrogen bonding interaction was observed between host–guest. The MOF exhibited solvatochromism and naked eye colorimetric transition for small molecules. An interesting problem i.e. hydrocarbon separation requires detection of types of hydrocarbons in an aromatic mixture. For this, Dong and co-workers have synthesized a Cd based MOF having formula $\mathrm{CdL}_{2}(\mathsf{O T s})_{2}$ wherein $\mathrm{L}=1{,}2$ -bis[3-(pyridine-4-yl)phenoxy]ethane, ${\mathsf{O T S}}={\mathsf{p}}$ -toluenesulfonate anion [232]. The MOF was interpenetrated in nature containing square like hydrophobic channels of dimensions $8\times9\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ . The guest free phase adsorbed reversibly halobenzenes like Ph-F, Ph-Cl, Ph-Br, Ph-I with affinity order Ph$\mathrm{I}>\mathrm{Ph-Br}>\mathrm{Ph-Cl}>\mathrm{Ph-F}$ . This inclusion of halobenzenes was directly correlated to the differential guest emission observed. The molecular packing of guests between the framework has been directly linked to the increase in photoluminescent emission intensity observed via increased $\pi\mathrm{-}\pi^{\ast}$ transition. Anion recognition is an important area of investigation as it plays central role in the biological and chemical processes. To explore the domain of MOFs in the area of anion recognition, the same group extended the study via synthesizing a cationic porous fluorene based MOF. The molecular formula of the compound was found as $([\mathsf{C u L}_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}0)_{0.5}]$ $\mathrm{(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3.25(C H_{2}C l_{2})}$ $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}0\mathrm{H}){\cdot}0{\cdot}.5\mathrm{H}_{2}0\rangle$ where $\mathsf{L}=4,4^{\prime}$ -(9,9-dibutyl$9H$ -fluorene-2,7-diyl)dipyridine [233]. Structural features of this
MOF consist of one dimensional channels with nitrate anion aligned through the channel. The authors investigated the anion exchange ability of this framework by dipping the desolvated framework in the aqueous solution of NaCl, KBr, KI, KSCN and ${\mathsf{N a N}}_{3}$ . A rapid visible colorimetric transition was observed and the inclusion of anions was monitored by FT-IR spectroscopy. FT-IR analysis showed characteristic peaks for both nitrate and respective inclusion anion from which it was confirmed partial exchange was occurring. They performed the competing studies and observed a trend for selectivity that followed the Hofmeister bias mostly.
Ethylene is an important precursor in the petrochemical industry and during the conversion of the hydrocarbons to ethylene, acetylene is also produced as a by product. The recognition of acetylene is quite crucial as it acts a poison during the production of polyethylene. The same group synthesized a $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ based MOF with molecular formula $[\mathrm{H}_{2}0\subset\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{L})_{2}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ ; wherein ${\mathrm{L}}=1$ -benzimidazo lyl-3,5-bis(4-pyridyl)benzene] (Fig. 33) [234]. The crystals exhibited a specific response towards ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ i.e the red-brown color of the crystals changed to yellowish green in a 98:2 feed mixture of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}/C_{2}H_{2}}$ . The ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ inclusion within the framework has been characterized via SC-XRD studies wherein weak host–guest interaction stabilize the guest. This direct inclusion of ${\mathsf{C}}_{2}{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ provided a visual colorimetric transition.
The same group illustrated in another report sensing of toxic intermediate p-Benzoquinone (Q) which is widely used in chemical industries and leads to irritation in eyes, skin dermatitis, erythema etc. was studied [235]. The sensing of Q was performed by using a porous 3D MOF with molecular formula $\mathsf{H}_{2}0\subset\mathsf{C d}$ $(\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}$ wherein ${\mathrm{L}}=4$ -amino-3,5-bis(4-pyridyl-3-phenyl)-1,2,4 -triazole.The adsorption of Q within the cavities of the framework was characterized using single crystal study and a direct colorimetric transition was observed from colourless to brown after inclusion of p-benzoquinone (Q). The direct influence of $\mathsf{Q}$ inclusion was observed on the photoluminescence profile as it led to quenching of the luminescence after formation of the radical ion pair state.
## 4.4. Catalysis
MOFs offering molecular engineering and tunability render the required level of control in developing efficient heterogeneous catalysts. Decorated with metal clusters or secondary building units (SBUs) with a combination of high porosity and surface area MOFs have emerged as a new paradigm in heterogeneous catalysis. Recent studies suggest that MOFs made from N-donor based organic linkers can offer tremendous advantages as alternative from some of the contemporary homogeneous catalysts. In one of the early reports documented Ogura, Fujita and co-workers explored a 2D square framework $\{\lbrack\mathsf{C d}(4,4^{\prime}\ –\mathsf{b p y})_{2}](\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}\},$ (1) for its efficacy in catalyzing cyanosilylation reaction [6]. The authors note that when treated with benzaldehyde and cyanotrimethylsilane in presence of 1 produced 2-(trimethylsiloxy)phenylacetoni trile in good yield $(77\%)$ . Additionally shape and size specificity was also observed to influence the catalytic process.
1-Butene which is a dimerized product of ethylene is an important chemical commodity in the industry for polyolefin synthesis. Conventional production of 1-butene involves homogeneous catalysis (AlphaButol process) however the alternative heterogeneous catalysts suffer either in activity or selectivity in producing high purity 1-butene.
Recently Dinc and co-workers came up with a superior porous MOF catalyst Ni-MFU-4l (MFU – Metal-organic framework UlmUniversity) for ethylene dimerization based on a well-defined and isolated active Ni(II) site via a mild cation exchange on MFU4l $(\mathrm{MFU-}4l=\mathrm{Zn}_{5}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}(\mathrm{BTDD})_{3}$ [236–238] (Fig. 34). The porous catalyst was inspired from an existing homogeneous catalyst $\mathrm{[TpMesNi]^{+}}$ $\mathrm{(TpMes=HB} $ (3-mesitylpyrazolyl)3) having comparable structural features. Ni $(10\%)$ -MFU-4l $(\mathsf{N i};Z\mathsf{n}\sim1;9)$ yielded remarkable performance in ethylene dimerization in presence of methylaluminoxane (MAO) with turnover frequency amounting to $21,000\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ and a staggering $94.9\%$ production of butenes along with minimal amount of hexanes $(4.8\%)$ , traces of polyethylene $0.3\%$ by weight) and and no higher oligomers. Further, an optimized condition of 100 equiv MAO $\mathrm{~\boldmath~\omega~}_{0}\circ_{\mathsf{C}}$ and 50 bar pressure) produced a TOF value of $22{,}600\ensuremath{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}$ with $98.4\%$ selectivity for butenes surpassing all reported active heterogeneous catalysts. The authors note that the parent MOF (Zn analogue) failed to show such catalytic activity. Additionally it was also found several factors like temperature, reaction pressure, aluminum activator and equivalents of MAO used had dramatic effects on the ethylene dimerization activity (TOF), selectivity and overall 1-butene production. A Cossee-Arlman mechanism was suggested to be operative for this reaction based on the first order pressure dependency of NiMFU-4l below 25 bar. The superior catalytic performance of the catalyst compared to [TpMesNi]+ was attributed to the combined balance in active site accessibility as well as the pore-induced shape and size selectivity.
In a follow up work Dinc and co-workers went a step ahead in their achievement and were able to establish conclusive mechanistic evidence for ethylene dimerization in Ni-MFU-4l [239] (Fig. 35). A series of systematic experiments involving isotope labeling, molecular probes as well as theoretical DFT calculation manifested Cossee-Arlman mechanism to be operative in the catalytic transformation. Interestingly, the authors also provided a blueprint of utilizing tools of organometallic chemistry for unraveling reaction mechanism in porous materials.
Extending their study Dinc and co-workers probed Ni-MFU-4l (1) obtained via ion exchange from $\mathrm{Zn}_{5}\mathrm{Cl}_{4}(\mathrm{BTDD})_{3}$ (MFU-4l) as a heterogeneous catalyst for selective dimerization of propylene [240]. The catalyst displayed C6 regioselectivity that can be interpreted adopting the mechanistic resemblance from the Ni(II) trispyrazolylborate complexes occurring via 2,1-insertion as well as slow product reinsertion.
Taking a cue from their previous work the same group extended their studies in olefin polymerization utilizing the same MOF $Z\mathrm{n}_{5}.$ - $\mathrm{Cl}_{4}(\mathrm{BTDD})_{3}$ , .(MFU-4 l) via cation exchange involving late transition metals (Ti and Cr) [241]. All four heterogeneous catalysts derived from the parent MOF displayed effective olefin polymerization with TOF ranging from 1300 to $15000{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}$ upon treatment with MAO or triethylaluminum in toluene and pressurized ethylene (40 bar). The resuting polymeric products were thoroughly characterized viz., high temperature gel permeation chromatography and showed good crystallinity as well as characteristic HDPE. Low polydispersities (5 for $\operatorname{Cr}(\operatorname{III})$ -MFU-4l to 2.0 for Ti(IV)-MFU-4l) suggested a single-site catalytic activity in operation. The authors validated the selectivity of the catalysts taking the Ti(III)-MFU-4l as a model and observed that TOF decreases in proportion with pressure while $\mathbf{M}_{\mathrm{N}}$ (number-average molecular weight) of the polymer products consequently increased. This type of pressure dependency was suggestive towards Cossee-Arlman mechanism involved in the polymerization. The superiority of single-site catalyst was further substantiated with its utility in copolymerization reaction between ethylene and propylene leading to the formation of copolymers in every case. Additionally morphological control over the resulting polymer was also achieved for the MOF catalysts.
In another work, Li, Zhou and co-workers expanded the horizon of MOF based heterogeneous catalysis by utilizing a rare base stable porphyrin containing MOF PCN-602(Mn) isoreticular to its Ni analogue $[\mathrm{Ni}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{TPPP-Ni})_{3}]$ (PCN-602(Ni)) (where $\mathrm{TPPP}=5,10,15,20$ -tetrakis(4-(1H-pyrazol-4-yl)-phenyl)porphyrin) [242]. Crafted from $[\mathrm{Ni}_{8}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\mathrm{P}\mathrm{z}_{12}]$ ( $\mathrm{P}z=$ pyrazolate) cluster both the MOFs displayed extraordinary chemical stability $\mathtt{\backslash p H4-}$ 14) with astounding resistance towards strongly coordinating anions such as $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ , $\mathsf{C O}_{3}^{2-}$ , and $\mathsf{P O}_{4}^{3-}$ . Adding to its remarkable stability PCN-602(Mn) illustrated phenomenal catalytic performance in
C–H bond halogenations outperforming previous Mn-porphyrin based homogeneous catalysts. The authors reported an excellent $93\%$ yield of chlorocyclohexane from cyclohexane at very short time $(2\mathsf{h})$ in a more environmentally benign solvent (acetone) at room temperature. Comparatively the homogeneous catalyst Mn (TPP)Cl (herein, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TPP}=$ meso-tetraphenylporphyrin) under similar conditions could only produce $8\%$ yield. Further PCN-602(Mn) retained $92\%$ of its activity even after regenerating for three consecutive cycles. Additionally the effect of pore-window size on the catalytic performance was systematically explored by comparing the performance with PCN-601(Mn) having smaller window. PCN-601(Mn) yielded only $15\%$ of the product even after prolonged reaction time $(10\mathrm{h})$ . The scope for PCN-602(Mn) catalyst was extended to cyclopentane and alkylane substrates with very good yields. Overall the superior performance of PCN-602(Mn) was attributed in trading off balance in the cavity size and robustness of the framework.
Liu, Dong and co-workers exploited the porosity of a Ni(II) MOF 2MeOH $0.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{0}\subset\mathrm{NiL}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}.$ ) (compound 1)(where $\mathrm{L}=1,1^{\prime}$ -(9,9-dime thyl-9H-fluorene-2,7-diyl)bis(1H-1,2,4-triazole) to generate a guest encapsulated composite MOF based heterogeneous catalyst for sliylcyanation of aromatic aldehydes. 1 contained octahedral $\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II})$ centers with four N-triazole donors and two axially bound ${\mathsf{C l}}^{-}$ anions [243]. The 2D net had rhombic channels (dimensions ${\sim}14\times10\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2})$ running along crystallographic $c$ axis. Upon exposure to molecular $\mathsf{I}_{2}\mathsf{\Omega}^{1}$ was converted to a new host–guest framework $0.75\mathrm{I}_{2}\subset\mathrm{NiL}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ (3) in a SC to SC manner. Compound 3 $(3.3\mathrm{mol\%})$ 1 when treated with benzaldehyde and trimethylsilylcyanide (TMSCN) afforded the trimethylsilylated cyanohydrins in very good yields $(97\%)$ at room temperature within $30\mathrm{min}$ . Notably this catalyst performed under solvent free conditions and being insoluble in the substrates was available for regeneration up to four cycles with delayed reaction time in the successive cycles. While the iodine free Ni(II)-MOF (2) was catalytically active it suffered from poor yields. Additionally the authors speculated that the catalysis may be occurring on the surface of the MOF by using larger aromatic aldehydes as substrates which was also supported from the XPS data.
Dong and co-workers reported a new $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ MOF $\langle[\mathsf{C u(L)}](\mathsf{S0}_{3}-$ $\mathrm{CF}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}2(\mathrm{H}_{2}0))$ (MOF-1) (wherein $\mathsf{I}=4,4^{\prime}$ -( $\cdot6{,}6^{\prime}$ -bis(pyridin-3-ylme thoxy)-[1,10-biphenyl]- $^{.3,3^{\prime}}$ -diyl)dipyridine) with available open metal sites and studied its efficacy towards low temperature water oxidation [244] (Fig. 36). MOF-1 had a twofold interpenetrated 3D framework with tubular nanochannels housing triflate anions and water molecules. Also additional ellipse-like pores of ca. $15\times10\mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{2}$ was suitable for small molecules to access the open metal sites. The authors note that when Cu (II) MOF-1 was sealed and heated at $150^{\circ}\mathsf C$ in presence of water for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ it gets converted to MOF-2 $([\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})](\mathsf{S O}_{3}\mathsf{C F}_{3})_{3}.2.1\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ with detectable visual color change. Interestingly enough apart from $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ being converted to $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ blustering bubbles released from the reaction mixture that was identified as oxygen evolved from water during the reaction. $0_{2}$ evolution was also validated with gas chromatography with $72.3\%$ yield. The thermochemical oxidation of water at low temperature was also monitored using an $0_{2}$ probe, a ferrocenyl-fulvene molecule $(\mathsf{C}_{39}\mathsf{H}_{36}\mathsf{N}_{2}\mathsf{F e}_{2})$ via isotope labeling. Combining $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{16}\mathrm{O}$ as well as $\mathrm{H}_{2}^{18}\mathrm{O}$ with MOF-1 resulted in formation of the deep green oxidized product of ferrocenyl-cyclopentenone species $(\mathsf{C}_{39}\mathsf{H}_{34}\mathsf{N}_{20}\mathsf{F e}_{2})$ with both $^{18}{\sf O}_{2}$ and $^{16}\dot{0}_{2}$ being trapped by the probe. ESR measurements were carried out to gain insight regarding the mechanism and it was suggested that the uncoordinated Cu (II) sites acts as an activator for water molecules that subsequently undergoes redox reaction to produce OH and MOF-2. Thus produced hydroxyl radical coupling creates $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ which dissociates to give $0_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ respectively. The same group also reported a ${\sf C o}({\sf I I})$ MOF 1 $\mathrm{\langle([Co_{3}L(C H_{3}O H)_{0.5}(H_{2}O)_{0.5}(P T A)_{2.5}(O A c)]}$ solvent)) (where $\mathsf{L}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -((1-ethyl-1H-imidazole-4,5-diyl)bis(4,1-pheny lene))dipyridine and $\mathrm{PT}\mathsf{A}=\mathsf{p}$ -phthalic acid) as an oxidation catalyst for organic molecules [245]. The parent MOF could undergo reversible MeOH exchange in a SC to SC pathway to give 2 ( $\mathrm{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathrm{L}$ $(\mathrm{PTA})_{2.5}(\mathrm{OAc})]$ solvent) with open Co (II) sites. Compound 2 was employed for oxidation of organic molecules in sovent free conditions. The heterogeneous catalyst was found to be recyclable and the role of uncoordinated Co (II) centers was also investigated.
Cirujano and co-workers reported a $\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II})$ pyrazolate based MOF as a solid matrix embedded with homogeneously dispersed [Pd $\left(\mathsf{N H}_{3}\right)_{4}]^{2+}$ species for hydroamination reactions [246]. The $\operatorname{K@}$ NiBDP MOF with formula $\mathrm{K[Ni_{8}(O H)_{3}(E t O)_{3}(B D P\_X)_{5.5}]}$ (Where ${\big\boldsymbol{\mathrm{X}}}={\mathrm{H}} $ and $\mathsf{B D P}=1,4$ -bis(pyrazol-4-yl))with extra framework cations was exchanged for highly active $\mathrm{[Pd(NH_{3})_{4}]^{2+}}$ to obtain Pd@NiBDP as a heterogeneous catalyst. Under optimized conditions the catalyst showed a conversion rate of $80\%$ and a selectivity of $64\%$ taking 2-ethynylaniline as model compound. This activity was superior compared to $\mathrm{[Pd(NH_{3})_{4}]C l_{2}}$ salt as well as precursor $\operatorname{K@}$ NiBDP and $\mathsf{C u@N i B D P}$ (Cu exchanged MOF) that gave a conversion below $20\%$ . A comparison in the intramolecular hydroamination reaction involving phenylacetylene and aniline revealed a better selectivity achieved by $\operatorname{Pd}\mathscr{@}$ NiBDP $(68\%)$ than that of its zeolite analogue ${\mathsf{P d@H Y}}$ $(13\%)$ . The authors identified $[\mathrm{Pd}(\mathrm{NH}_{3})_{4}]^{2+}$ as the active catalytic site confirmed by the suppressed activity of the catalyst upon heat treatment $200^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ overnight) resulting in agglomeration of the reduced $\operatorname{Pd}(0)$ nanoparticles. Further the catalyst was reusable without major loss in the activity while the proposed mechanism involved coordination of the Pd sites with the substrate triple bond.
## 4.5. Magnetism
Absolute command at molecular level governed by an external stimulus is of paramount interest in engineering next generation molecular devices viz., molecular switches, memory devices, logic gates etc. N-donor ligand based coordination compounds have commanded remarkable attention for applications as magnetic materials over the years. In particular, on account of its application toward information technology and optical switches spin crossover (SCO) have interested researchers for long time. Discovered back in 1931 the essence of SCO phenomenon lies in low spin (LS) to high spin (HS) transition stimulated by temperature, pressure, light or electromagnetic radiation etc., that results in sharp changes in geometry, chromism as well as magnetism. Thus materials illustrating SCO behavior especially with hysteresis or spin transition above room temperature are potential candidates for molecule based spintronics and sensors. In the recent past N-donor based linkers have served as an ideal platform for crafting diverse SCO materials with unique magnetic properties; the current discourse intends to discuss magnetic properties of MOF-compounds. In this context, a well-known report by Kepert and co-workers serves as a blueprint where SCO centers was realized inside a nanoporous framework scaffold [247]. The doubly interpenetrated $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathsf{a z p y})_{4}$ $(\mathsf{N C S})_{4}$ EtOH) (Where azpy $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ azpyis trans-4,4-azopyridine) MOF 1 (EtOH) that underwent ‘‘half-spin” transition beyond $150\mathrm{K}$ (Fig. 37a-b). On the contrary the evacuated phase with no guest ethanol molecules did not show any SCO thus confirming the crucial role of H-bonding guest molecules having direct electronic influence over Fe(II) centers. Subsequently, this domain explored by other groups utilizing N-donor linker based compounds for such SCO studies [248–250]. In this regard, recent advancements have been discussed below.
Zubieta and co-workers prepared a 3D microporous MOF $[{\mathsf{C o}}_{2}-$ $\left(\mathrm{H}_{0.67}\mathrm{bdt}\right)_{3}]{\cdot20\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ $(1{\cdot}20\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ [where $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bdt $\mathbf{\Sigma}=5{,}5^{\prime}$ -(1,4-phenylene) bis(1H-tetrazole] that demonstrated single chain magnetic behavior along with a reversible change upon dehydration and rehydration [251]. The 3D structure is crafted from {Co(tetrazolate)n chains that extend parallel to the crystallographic $a$ axis. Further between the chains each Co(II) centers are in octahedral coordination bonded through nitrogen donors coming from six bdt ligands. MOF $1{\cdot}20\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ showed a maximum $\chi\mathrm{T}$ value of 3.7 emumol $^{-1}\mathsf{K}$ at $300\mathrm{K}$ with a steady decrease with lowering of temperature. The authors observed that the $\chi\mathrm{T}$ value was significantly higher than that of spin only case $\langle\chi_{\mathrm{co}}T=1.875$ emumo ${}^{-1}\mathrm{K},$ $S=3/2\AA,$ and attributed this to orbital contribution. Interestingly the polycrystalline sample exhibited a completely different magnetism from $1{\cdot}20\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ . The $\chi\mathrm{T}$ value at room temperature was found to be 1 emumol $^{-1}\mathsf{K}$ indicating more than $80\%$ of Co ions being in LS state. Also for this compound the decreasing $\chi\mathrm{T}$ value reached a minimum at $2\mathrm{K}$ as a consequence of antiferromagnetic interactions. Additionally zero-field AC susceptibility across different frequencies gave a broad out-of-phase signal below $5\mathrm{K}$ while the dry sample did not show any such out-of-phase AC signal. The Cole-Cole plot gave a semicircular graph validating the single-chain magnetic nature with a $\alpha$ value of 0.1. The compound was found to exhibit thermal and hydrolytic stability. Kepert and co-workers integrated spin crossover (SCO) centers in a new Hofmann type 3D metal organic framework material that displayed a unique three step SCO conversion existing both on cooling and warming while showing a broad thermal hysteresis [252]. The 3D pillared MOF $\mathrm{\Delta[Fe(dpsme)Pt(CN)_{4}]{\cdot}2/3}$ dpsmexEtOH $\mathrm{\cdotyH}_{2}0$ (1; dpsme $\mathbf{\Psi}=4,4^{\prime}$ di(pyridylthio)methane; $\mathrm{EtOH}=$ ethanol) contains rectangular channels occupied with disordered uncoordinated dpsme ligands $\pi$ stacked with the coordinated pyridyl units. Compound 1 showed a static $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ at ca. $3.30\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{K}\mathrm{m}0^{1^{-1}}$ suggesting HS, $S=2\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}$ sites exclusively. Upon lowering the temperature the $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}\mathrm{T}$ drops to a minimum value of $0.06\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{Kmol}^{-1}$ beyond $110\mathrm{K}$ with three distinctive steps which is coherent with only $\mathsf{L S\ F e^{I I}}$ sites $(S=0)$ . The first transition at $138\mathrm{K}$ illustrated a sharp drop and arrived to a plateau yielding a $50\%$ change of the spin state in the $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers. Subsequently, second and third conversion took place at 127 and $115\mathrm{K}$ respectively leading to a complete transition into $\mathsf{L S\ F e^{\mathrm{II}}}$ via consecutive spin conversions. Compound 1 also exhibited light-induced spin switching in accordance to LIESST and reverse-LIESST effect. Irradiating the LS state with $532\mathrm{nm}$ green light yielded a partial photo-converted HS state (kinetically stable in absence of light) while at $10\mathrm{K}$ this can be turned back with illuminating at $830\mathrm{nm}$ . The authors speculated that the complex SCO behavior is ascribed to the number of subtle alterations in which the MOF switches between oriented HS and LS centers. This MOF also showed pronounced thermochromism in association with spin transition from yellow (room temperature) to dark red (liquid nitrogen temperature). This MOF was further explored by Chastanet and co-workers by probing in detail the light-induced excited spin-state trapping (LIESST) effect properties [253]. The authors carried out detailed photomagnetic characterization as well as the kinetics of high spin to low spin relaxation was also reported. In another work, Kepert and co-workers utilized covalent postsynthetic modification for comparative study of the SCO behavior in a pair of MOFs [254]. The parent MOF [Fe(bipytz) $\left|\big(\mathsf{A u}(\mathsf{C N})_{2}\big)_{2}\right]$ (1) (bipytz $\mathbf{\delta}=3{,}6$ -bis(4-pyridyl)-1,2,4,5-tetrazine) was converted to its analogues phase [Fe (bipydz) $\mathsf{\Gamma}\big|\big(\mathsf{A u}(\mathsf{C N})_{2}\big)_{2}\big]$ (2) (bipydz $\mathbf{\varepsilon}=3{,}6{\cdot}$ - bis(4-pyridyl)-1,2-diazine) in a SC to SC manner. Magnetic susceptibility plot revealed a complete SCO for 1 where the $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}\mathrm{T}$ decreases from $3.3\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{Kmol}^{-1}$ at 290 K $(\mathrm{HS\Fe^{\mathrm{II}})}$ to $0.01\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{Kmol}^{-1}$ $(\mathrm{LS\Fe^{\mathrm{II}})}$ at $250\mathrm{K}$ accompanying a sharp $4\mathrm{K}$ hysteresis. Remarkably compound 2 demonstrated marked deviation in its SCO behavior compared to that of 1. Three distinct changes were
Kepert, Neville and co-workers synthesized a new Hofmanntype framework scaffold showing multi step SCO that is intrinsically frustrated resulting from antagonistic interactions (Fig. 38) [258]. The pseudo-3-D framework $\mathrm{[Fe^{II}(b z t r z)_{2}(P d^{I I}(C N)_{4})]{\cdot n}}$ (guest), 1 $(\mathrm{H}_{2}0,\mathrm{EtOH})$ $(\mathsf{b}2\mathsf{t r}2=(\mathsf{E})-1$ -phenyl-N-(1,2,4-triazol-4-yl) methanimine) was decorated with bztrz ligands from neighboring layers arranged in a ‘‘head to tail” manner. Also there were two different ligand spacings where pairs of ligands in close proximity interact in various H-bonding interactions while greater ligand spacing holds guest molecules. As-synthesized 1 $\mathrm{'}\mathrm{H}_{2}0,\mathrm{Et}0\mathrm{H})$ was further converted to two partially desolvated $\boldsymbol{1}.3\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0}$ ; $1{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ analogues. Parent material 1 $(\mathsf{H}_{2}0,\mathsf{E t O H})$ displayed a one step SCO accompanying thermal hysteresis while $\boldsymbol{1}.3\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0}$ showed a twostep spin transition where the higher temperature hysteresis loop represented a $50\%\mathrm{HS}\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ to LS changeover and lower temperature hysteresis loop indicated $25\%$ of $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers of being in HS state. Interestingly $\boldsymbol{1}{\cdot}\boldsymbol{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0}$ yielded a three-step SCO conversion with characteristic two hysteretic loops. Probing into the detailed insight of this unusual SCO behavior originating from subtle guest adjustments unveiled that guest exchange in the parent material was imperative in harnessing an array of SCO behavior initiated from competing ferro- and antiferro-elastic interactions in the crystal lattice.
Precise manipulation in the atomic behavior by external stimuli is of paramount interest in generating sophisticated responsive systems. In this regard, recently Kepert and co-workers reported an ultra-flexible framework $\mathrm{\Delta[Fe(bpac)(Au(CN)_{2})_{2}]{\cdot2E t O H}}$ (bpac $=1{,}2$ -bis $4^{\prime}$ -pyridyl)acetylene),denoted as [Fe], showcasing SCO induced striking mechanical changes in the crystal lattice (Fig. 39) [259]. Compound [Fe] attains reversible spin conversion of HS to LS at $\mathrm{T}_{1/2}^{\perp}=221\ \mathrm{K}$ (cooling) and LS to HS transition takes place at $T_{1/2}^{\downarrow}=22\dot{6}K$ (heating). Single crystal data from 190 and $240\mathrm{K}$ respectively disclosed very unusual parametric $\mathbf{\Delta}\cdot\Delta\mathbf{a}=-0.850$ (3) Å and $\Delta{\ b}=+1.610(5)\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ ) change that cannot be solely ascribed to the increasing Fe–N bond lengths. It was observed that phase discontinuity emerged at ${\sim}215$ and ${\sim}221\mathrm{\K}$ with abrupt changes in the lattice parameters $(\Delta\mathbf{a}=-0.606(1)\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and $\Delta{\mathsf{b}}=+1.343(2)\mathring{\mathsf{A}}_{.}^{}$ in the cooling and heating cycles respectively. Further, this unique scissor-like flexing in the crystal lattice is attributed to a synergistic combination of weak intermolecular interactions and alteration in the Au  Fe  Au angle leading to expansion and contraction along $a$ -axis and $b$ -axis respectively. The authors also did a control experiment involving metal dilution by incorporating ${\mathsf{N i}}^{\mathrm{II}}$ as a dopant in a $[\mathsf{F e}_{\mathbf{x}}\mathsf{N i}_{1-\mathbf{x}}]$ series and observed more gradual SCO over wide temperature range. Linear coefficients of thermal expansion having magnitude of $\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\propto-3200\times10^{-6}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ , $\mathrm{\alpha\times_{b}}=+5200\times10^{-6}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ and $\mathcal{U}_{c}=+1500\times10^{-6}\mathrm{K}^{-1}$ was reported $\cdot a,b$ and $c$ axes respectively) for $\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{0.84}\mathrm{Ni}_{0.16}\right]$ at $215\mathrm{K}.$ It was observed that increment in ${\bf N i}^{\mathrm{II}}$ dilution also broadens linear thermal expansion temperature range disrupting the lattice cooperativity. Kepert, Neville and co-workers introduced two 2D Hofmann-like materials $[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{saltrz})_{6}(\mathrm{M}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{CN})_{4})_{3}].$ ${\8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}{0})$ $(\mathsf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}=\mathsf{P d}(1^{\mathsf{P d}})$ , Pt $(1^{\mathsf{P t}})$ ; saltrz $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ (E)-2-(((4H-1,2,4-triazol-4-yl) imino)methyl)phenol) showcasing a rare four-step SCO behavior [260]. Temperature dependent magnetic susceptibility for $1^{\mathrm{{Pt}}}$ presented a decrease in $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ from ca. $3.38\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\mathrm{K}$ to $2.12\mathrm{\bar{cm}}^{3}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\mathrm{K}$ that further diminished gradually corresponding to the conversion of HS $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centers to LS spin states having different HS fractions upon cooling from room temperature to below 90 K. $1^{\mathrm{Pd}}$ also exhibited a four-step spin transition when irradiated with a light of $\lambda=510\mathrm{nm}$ at $10\mathrm{K}.$ This photo-excitation process was easily recycled applying ‘‘on” and ‘‘off” at $10\mathrm{K}$ via changing the wavelengths at $510\mathrm{nm}$ (LIESST effect) to $830\mathrm{nm}$ (reverse-LIESST effect). The authors reasoned that the synergy between lattice cooperativity and antagonistic solid state interactions was imperative behind this unique and rare existence of five structurally distinct spin states. The same group extended their studies by synthesizing a new 2D Hofmann-like material $\mathrm{\small{[Fe(proptrz)_{2}M(C N)_{4}]}}.2\mathrm{\scriptsize{H_{2}O}}$ $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{Pd}$ or Pt; 1 (Pd). $2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , $1(\mathrm{Pt}).2\mathrm{H}_{2}0\rangle$ (wherein (proptrz $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ (E)-3-phenyl-N-(4H-1,2, 4-triazol-4-yl)prop-2-yn-1-imine) based on a novel triazole functionalized linker [261]. Hydrogen bonding as well as aromatic interacting moiety was strategically tethered to facilitate spin communicating pathways exploiting the host-host and host-guest interactions. The authors were able to realize the very first 2D Hofmann-like framework scaffolds that displayed ambient temperature SCO behavior accompanying wide thermal hysteresis. Pedersion and co-workers applied reticular chemistry to obtain a layered 2D coordination solid $\mathrm{CrCl_{2}(p y z)_{2}(p y z=}$ pyrazine) displaying longrange magnetic ordering as well as high electronic conductivity (Fig. 40) [262]. A room temperature electronic conductivity of $32\mathrm{m}\mathrm{Scm}^{-1}$ along with a ferromagnetic behavior below ${\sim}55\mathrm{K}$ was observerd for this redox-active compound. The $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ value $(2.7\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ - $\mathrm{Kmol}^{-1}$ at 400 K) for $\mathrm{{CrCl}}_{2}(\mathfrak{p y}z)_{2}$ showed substantial increment with decrease in the temperature with an abrupt increase at ${\sim}55\mathrm{K}$ indicating a magnetic phase transformation. The authors attributed this to the firm magnetic interactions present between the spin carriers. The phase change was further validated with alternating current susceptibility measurements. Additionally DFT calculations were performed using a model fragment of trans- $\cdot[\mathrm{CrCl_{2}(p y z)}_{4}]$ revealing prominent spin density on the suspended pyrazines with rich amount of $\pi-d$ conjugation as well as strong antiferromagnetic $\mathsf{C r}^{3+}$ –pyz radical exchange coupling. The electrical conductivity measured at different temperatures suggested a hopping-based mechanism to be operating for transport.
Very recently Ruiz-Molina and co-workers demonstrated thermochromic polymer films grown from delaminating a 2D SCO coordination polymer $\{\mathrm{[Fe(L1)_{2}](C l O_{4})_{2}}\}_{\propto}$ (1) $\mathrm{{\dot{L}=}}$ tris(2-(1H-tetra zol-1-yl)ethyl)-amine) [263]. Liquid phase exfoliation in water medium was used as a top-down approach to obtain flakes with controlled thickness ( $\cdot1{-}2\mathrm{nm})$ with no compromise in the chemical and SCO behavior from the bulk. The SCO behaviors of the exfoliated flakes were investigated with UV–vis spectroscopy with different cooling and heating cycles. The spin transition was associated with naked eye detectable thermochromism going from colorless (HS) to pink-violet (LS). Also ab initio DFT calculations were also performed to gain insight for transformation of the SCO behavior from bulk to the flakes. Materials embedded with dual properties of SCO and fluorescence are well suited for formulating biosensors for nanothermometry as well as fluorescence microscopy. To this point, very recently Kӧhler, Weber and coworkers reported a 1D SCO coordination polymer [Fe-(L1)(bipy)]n (4) (where $\mathsf{L}=\mathsf{a}\ \mathsf{N}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}^{2-}$ coordinating Schiff-base like ligand bearing a phenazine fluorophore and bipy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ bipyridine) illustrating remarkable correlation in emission properties and spin switching [264]. The authors were unable to obtain single crystals of 4 while the crystal structure of a dimer $\{(\mathsf{\Gamma}\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{b i p y})[\mathsf{F e L1}(\mathsf{M e O H})]_{2}\}{\mathrm{.2MeOH}}$ (3) was evaluated in detail. Magnetic measurements revealed compound 3 to be in HS state in 50 to $400\mathrm{K}$ temperature range with $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}\mathrm{T}$ value amounting to $7.40\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{Kmol}^{-1}$ . On the contrary, 4 presented a unique temperature dependence in the $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}\mathrm{T}$ value with a wide thermal hysteresis of $48\mathrm{K}$ above room temperature. Upon heating compound 4 from room temperature $(\mathcal{X}_{\mathrm{M}}\mathrm{T}=0.095\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ - $\mathrm{Kmol}^{-1}.$ ) the LS iron (II) centers undergoes spin transition at $\mathrm{T}1/2\uparrow=380\mathrm{K}$ with the HS state having a $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ value of $3.11~\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ - $\mathrm{Kmol}^{-1}$ at $400\mathrm{K}.$ This hysteretic SCO behavior for 4 was intact a up to 5 cycles while the DSC measurements corroborated well with the magnetic behavior of 4. Additionally the authors note that the relative relative fluorescence intensity $\mathrm{PL}(\lambda_{2})/\mathrm{PL}(\lambda_{1})$ (where $\lambda_{1}=550\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{~\&~}\lambda_{2}=673\mathrm{nm})$ of compound 4 mirrors the change in its spin state. The correlation between the spin state and relative PL peak intensities, i.e, $\mathrm{PL}(\lambda_{2})/\mathrm{PL}(\lambda_{1})$ was further supported by plotting a variable temperature photoluminescence ratio graph that confirmed that the SCO behavior can be traced by fluorescence spectra. In a similar kind of work Real and co-workers reported the first 3D Hofmann type CP showcasing a synergistic SCO behavior along with luminescence [265]. The pyrene based Hofmann clathrate $\{\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{bpben})[\mathrm{Au}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}]\}@\mathrm{pyr}[\mathrm{1Fe}@\mathrm{pyr}]$ (bpben $=1{,}4$ -bis (4-pyridyl)benzene; pyr $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ pyrene). The multi-step SCO behavior of ${1}\mathsf{F e@p y r}$ can be tracked by emission spectroscopy. The origin of fluorescence in the compound was attribute to pyrene and exciplex emissions that were controlled by the spin state of $\mathsf{F e}^{\mathrm{II}}$ sites. The compound also exhibited reversible therchromism from pale-yellow (HS) to red (LS).
## 4.6. Water adsorption
Adsorption driven heat pumps provides an edge with its capability to function under low-grade energy viz., sunlight or low-temperature waste heat thus providing an energy economic process. Unlike zeolites or silica gels MOFs empowered with its unparalleled control over structure and property has prevailed in water sorption application owing to its high loading capacity across wide humidity range. Literature reports indicate that N-donor spacer containing MOFs distinctly stand out as one of the superior water sorption materials.
Dinca and co-workers systematically examined water adsorption properties in an isostructural series of pyrazolate based MOFs viz. $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{N D I-X})$ (X: -H, -NHEt, -SEt) [266]. The compound comprises of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ nodes in tetrahedral geometry and wide porous channels (16 Å). As anticipated for pyrazolate linkers, the authors found all the compounds to be hydrolytically stable. Water vapor adsorption isotherms recorded at $293\mathrm{K},$ showed sharp uptake at $\ensuremath{\mathrm{P}}/\ensuremath{\ensuremath{\mathrm{P}}_{0}}=0.4-$ 0.5, which suggested hydrophobic pore channels. Interestingly the uptake amounts were correlated to the hydrophobic features ( $\pi$ -character) of the secondary functional sites $-\mathrm{H}\ (0.00)<-\mathrm{NHEt}$ $\left(+0.08\right)<-S\mathrm{Et}$ $(+1.07)$ . To substantiate this claim further, the authors carried out post-synthetic modification of $Z n(\mathsf{N D I-S E t})$ to lead to formation of hydrophilic polar sites viz. -SOEt $(-1.04)$ and $-S0_{2}\mathrm{Et}$ (-1.09). The post-synthetic modification led to shifting of sharp opening for water uptake $(\mathrm{P}/\mathrm{P}_{0}=0.2–0.3$ for -SOEt and $_{0.3-0.4}$ for $-S0_{2}\mathrm{Et}^{\cdot}$ . This work emphasized the role of secondary functional sites and the power of post-synthetic modification to control molecular-scale properties.
The effect of pore character in water adsorption properties was further demonstrated by post-synthetic cation-exchange (Fig. 41) [267]. The authors employed $\mathrm{Zn_{5}C l_{4}(B T D D)_{3}}$ for the study which exhibited almost negligible water uptake between 0 and $65\%$ RH. Subsequently the compound adsorbs $1.05{\mathrm{g/g}}$ at $95\%$ RH which is among the highest uptake capacities by any material. The stability of the compound was verified by surface area measurements. The authors sought to replace tetrahedral $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ with ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ as the latter could expose an open metal site and the ion-exchange was feasible owing to presence of $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ on the peripheral part of the structure. As anticipated, the authors found a gradual shift in the onset pressure for water uptake corresponding to increasing content of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ in the framework. Interestingly, all the 3 doped phases had higher uptakes at lower pressure regions which was ascribed to the presence of open metal sites, but the total uptake capacities were almost similar to that of the parent framework.
The group further explored water sorption properties in a series of mesoporous azolate based MOFs viz. $\mathbf{M}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ (BTDD) (M – Mn, Co, Ni) (Fig. 42) [268]. The compounds bearing open metal sites, had significant uptake at lower pressure region which is substantially higher than several benchmark sorbents. Among the 3 studied compounds, Mn-MOF was found to have unusual pattern which was linked to structural disintegration. The other two compounds exhibited steep uptakes at $\ensuremath{\mathrm{P}}/\ensuremath{\ensuremath{\mathrm{P}}_{0}}=0.28$ . The Co-MOF had the highest uptake amount and was used for further studies. The authors simulated real conditions in desert regions and correlated the observed uptake patterns. Based on the calculations, it was estimated that the Co-MOF could capture $82\%$ (wt%) water at night, which was significantly higher than previously reported material. Zaworotko and co-workers explored water adsorption features in
4 compounds constructed from inorganic pillar anions viz. SIFSIX1-Cu, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i, SIFSIX-3-Ni and SIFSIX-14-Cu-i [269]. The isotherms for SIFSIX-1-Cu, SIFSIX-3-Ni and SIFSIX-14-Cu-i showed an interesting negative adsorption at $40-50\%$ RH. Detailed studies revealed that all 3 compounds underwent structural transformation associated with the unusual sorption profile. Among the MOFs, SIFSIX-1-Cu was found to undergo irreversible transformation to non-porous phase, while SIFSIX-3-Ni could be regenerated over multiple cycles. Unlike, these 3 compounds, SIFSIX-2-Cu-i displayed a type V uptake profile and remained unaffected structurally and retained porosity. Although SIFSIX-2-Cu-i and SIFSIX-14-Cu-i are isostructural in nature, the former was found to present much higher hydrolytic stability. Such insights contribute to the design principles guiding the synthesis of microporous solids for sorption applications.
Eddaoudi and co-workers explored water sorption properties in two fluorinated MOFs viz. $\mathrm{NiAlF}_{5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\sf O})(\mathrm{pyr})_{2}.2(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\sf O})$ (KAUST-8 or AlFFIVE-1-Ni; pyr – pyrazine) and $\mathrm{NiFeF}_{5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\bf O})({\bf p}{\bf y}{\bf r})_{2}{\cdot}2(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\bf O})$ (KAUST-9 or FeFIVE-1-Ni) (Fig. 43) [159]. The structural features were found to be analogous to SIFSIX series MOFs with coordinated water the inorganic pillars, which plays a key role in the sorption features of the MOFs. Both the compounds were found to present high thermal and hydrolytic stability. The compound exhibited very high uptake at low pressure $(\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}=0.05)$ with 22 and
$18\mathrm{wt\%}$ for KAUST-8 and KAUST-9 respectively. Interestingly the regeneration temperature was found to be significantly lower $(\sim105^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ than conventional sorbents $(\sim250^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ and recyclable performance. Detailed DFT calculations were carried out to understand the mechanistic details and breakthrough experiments were recorded to evaluate the performance in presence of common gases. Zaworotko’s group extended their investigations to a series of CMOMs (chiral metal-organic materials) viz. $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(S\mathrm{-man})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}](\mathsf{O T f})_{2}$ , $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(R\mathrm{-}2\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C l}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{m a n})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}](\mathsf{O T f})_{2},$ $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(R\mathrm{-}3\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C l}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{m a n})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}](\mathsf{O T f})_{2},$ , $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(R\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C l-m a n})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})_{3}](\mathsf{O T f})$ $[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(R\mathrm{-}4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{man})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})_{3}](\mathrm{OTf})_{2}$ , where man - mandelic acid [270]. Low temperature ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption revealed similar uptakes and surface areas for all the compounds, but water adsorption isotherms at room temperature resulted in noticeably different uptake profiles and amounts. While two compounds did not show any significant uptake, the remaining three compounds had filing up of the pore at different pressure points. Detailed characterization was carried out to understand the interactions, which yielded strong H-bond between water and triflate anions. Unlike previous examples where steep openings resulted in structural changes, only the pore cavities were reconfigured in the current case, for which the authors propose that these materials classify as 4th generation MOFs; in which the overall structure is robust, but the pores are adaptable and flexible to the guest accommodation.
## 4.7. Emerging applications
Recent trends have shown that MOFs are seeking importance in varied applications viz. ion/ electron conduction (proton conduction and Li-ion battery), oil-water separation, MOF glass, electrocatalyst (OER, HER), toxic gas capture some of which are mentioned below. Recently, design principles and crystal engineering towards more stable frameworks has led towards the development of nitrogen donor MOFs in terms of stability [71,271]. In this section, recent literature developments that have focused on nitrogen donor based MOFs have been discussed below.
Oil water separation is an important area of research owing to the large scale oil spills which directly impact the ecosystem. Existing sorbent materials utilized for the selective capture of hydrocarbons from water have a considerable affinity towards water as well. Hence, there is a need for the development of novel sorbents. Hydrophobic MOFs i.e. MOFs posing a hydrophobic surface which can selectively adsorb hydrocarbons have shown immense potential towards such selective sequestration. Li and co-workers synthesized a Copper based hydrophobic MOF having molecular formula viz. $\mathtt{C u}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ wherein $\mathsf{L}=3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -tetraethyl- $\cdot4,4^{\prime}{-}\flat$ ipyrazolate) [272]. This MOF represent a rare example of $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ based MOF posing an unprecedented topology accompanied with an exceptional thermal and chemical stability. Owing to the hydrophobic channels, this MOF selectively adsorbs solvents such as benzene $(179.9\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{kg})$ and $\mathfrak{n}$ -hexane $(148.2\mathrm{g/kg)}$ at low saturated pressure as compared to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ for which negligible uptake was observed. The practical aspect of this MOF was assessed by soaking activated phase of $\mathtt{C u}_{2}\mathtt{L}$ in an aqueous solution of trace C6-C8 hydrocarbons. Within $10\mathrm{min}$ of exposure of activated MOF to benzene, the concentration reduced to $2.2\%$ in water as monitored via UV–Vis spectroscopy thus performing excellent in terms of the kinetics. In the follow up work by Zhang and coworkers, a hydrophobic MAF i.e. metal-azolate framework RHO$\left[Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{L})_{2}\right]$ was fabricated using ${\mathrm{L}}=2$ -ethyl-imidazole. MAF-6 exhibited high surface area with a pore size of $18.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ bearing an aperture of $7.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ [273]. Negligible uptake was observed in the water sorption profile which can be ascribed to the repulsion of water molecules by the ethyl groups present at the surface. The contact angle was found to be $143^{\circ}$ which reinstated the hydrophobic nature of MAF-6. This hydrophobic nature of MAF was exploited further by sorption of hydrophobic analytes such as benzene, ethanol, methanol. The results confirmed hydrophobic character as benzene showed type IV profile with an appreciable uptake in low pressure range. Attributed to the differential surface interaction, MAF-6 also exhibited separation of hydrocarbons in gas-chromatography studies where retention time followed the following order ethylbenzene $>$ stryene.
Cleaner sources of energy are currently pursued extensively due to environmental concerns. Fuel cells have shown promise in this regard and comprehensive investigation is underway. Porous materials have made an impact in this field especially MOFs owing to their tenable nature. Ion conduction is quite crucial in fuel cells and the following detailed description highlights the N-donor linker based compounds and their studies towards achieving ionic/ proton conduction. In an early study by Navarro and co-workers they have fabricated fcu topology based MOF having formula $[\mathrm{Ni_{8}(O H)_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}(B D P\_X)_{6}}]$ wherein $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDP}\_\mathrm{X}=1{,}4$ -bis(pyrazol4-yl)benzene-4-X (N donor linker) with ${\big\vert}{\big.}\qquad{\big.}{\big\vert} $ (1), OH (2), $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ (3) [274]. Post synthetic treatment with KOH/EtOH led to the formation of hydroxide ions within the channels of the framework alongwith presence of missing linker defect. The framework maintained integrity post harsh treatment with KOH as corroborated by PXRD. The authors performed variable temperature AC impedance spectroscopy for 1,2 and 3 and parent framework. A remarkable increase in the conduction upto an order of four orders in magnitude was noted. This increase in conduction was attributed to hydrogen bonding network between the water molecules present within the pores and the hydroxide ions thus suggesting a possibility of Grotthuss mechanism.
In a recent study using a MOF derived from N-donor linker Eddaoudi and co-workers have investigated MOF based superprotonic conduction [275]. They synthesized a water stable 3D MOF viz. KAUST-7 i.e., having pcu topology wherein $(\mathsf{N b O F}_{5})^{2-}$ linkers bridged the 2D square grid Ni-(pyrazine $)_{2}$ (Fig. 44a). KAUST-7 transformed to KAUST-70 after exposure to $363\mathrm{K}$ and $95\%$ humidity. KAUST- ${\cdot}7^{\prime}$ displayed superprotonic conduction which was ascribed to presence of acidic protons at the metal center and an array of proton conduction channel formed via hydrogen bonded water molecules in the crystalline tunnel. Arrhenius plot revealed an activation energy of $0.19\mathrm{eV}$ suggesting grotthus mechanism i.e., water assisted proton transfer. The role of water was demonstrated by performing impedance measurements with an increasing humidity wherein conduction value increased dramatically from $2.9\times10^{-9}$ to $6.7\times10^{-3}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ hence confirming water mediated conduction (Fig. 44b). The material also exhibited an interesting transformation from superprotonic conductor $(\upsigma>10^{-2}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm})$ to an insulator $(\upsigma<10^{-10}\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{cm})$ upon dehydration. This drastic change in performance suggested that this material can serve as an impedance based humidity sensor. The experimental data was supported by molecular simulations which points out to a global rearrangement of hydrogen bond network thus forming the basis of excellent proton conducting pathways.
Solid state ion conduction is an emerging area of interest as liquid electrolytes lack robustness, processing difficulties. Solid state materials especially MOFs are seeking attention as ionic frameworks can be synthesized with ease especially N-donor based MOFs. In a recent work by Dinca and co-workers, a novel $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ based anionic MOF was synthesized using $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B T D D}$ [79]. The overall formula of MOF was $((\mathrm{CH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{NH_{2}})[\mathrm{Cu_{2}C l_{3}B T D D}]$  $(\mathrm{DMF})_{4}$ $(\mathsf{H}_{2}0)_{4.5}$ (MIT-20). Upon evacuation of the MOF post treatment via soxhlet extraction led to formation of neutral framework viz. MIT-20d (Fig. 45a). The authors showed a reversible phase transformation by treating MIT-20d by excess of metal halides. Li:Cu ratio was found as 1:2 via ICP-MS analysis. No leaching of the cation was confirmed by ICP-MS. Similarly, NaSCN and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MgBr}_{2}}$ were used to isolate ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ and $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ within the hexagonal voids. MIT-20-X $\langle\mathsf{X}=\mathsf{L i C l}$ , Na, Mg) were analyzed for ion conduction within the voids by alternating current (AC) impedance spectroscopy. Conductivity value was found as $1.8\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S/cm}$ , $1.3\times10^{-5}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ and $8.8\times10^{-7}\mathsf{S}/\mathrm{cm}$ for MIT-20-Na, MIT-20-LiCl and MIT-20-Mg respectively (Fig. 45b). The lower conductivity of $\mathsf{M g}^{2+}$ was ascribed to the higher charge being transported through the channels having conserved electric field. Authors loaded MIT-20-LiCl with excess of $\mathrm{LiBF}_{4}$ salt with an overall molecular formula as $\mathrm{Li_{0.9}}[\mathrm{Cu_{2}C l_{2}B r_{0.9}B T D D}].0.3\mathrm{LiBF_{4}}.9(\mathrm{PC})$ wherein ${\mathsf{P C}}=$ propylene carbonate. The inclusion of extraframework lithium ions led to a remarkable increase in conductivity value of $4.8\times10^{-4}\mathrm{S/cm}$ which affirmed the conduction through the $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions. The activation energy of was determined by variable temperature AC impedance data. The calculations suggested the activation energy of MIT-20- LiBF4 as $0.16\mathrm{eV}$ thus making this material a superionic conductor.
Electrical conductivity in MOFs has opened up novel avenues in the field of electrocatalyst, electrochromic devices etc. N-donor MOFs have shown prospect in this field and an initial investigation by the previous group reported N-donor MOF exhibiting metallic conductivity exceeding $800{\cal S}/\mathrm{cm}$ at room temperature. The authors synthesized microcrystalline powders of twodimensionally connected MOF viz. $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I B})_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H I B})_{2}$ using ${\mathrm{HIB}}=$ hexaiminobenzene [276]. The reason to chose this linker was owed to the smaller size which would increase the overlap of metal/ligand orbitals thus leading to possible metallic character.
The authors fabricated nanoparticles with dimensions less than $100\mathrm{nm}$ . Indexing of synchrotron PXRD revealed hexagonal unit cell and EXAFS revealed AB stacking. To understand the electronic band structure DFT calculations were performed. The density of states (DOS) calculations suggested significant contributions from Ni/Cu, C and N orbitals of the linker which lie at the Fermi level. This observation confirmed the high level of delocalization (conjugation) among the various orbitals. The bulk electrical properties were further analyzed on pelletized sample upon degassing and the sample was measured using van der Pauw method in vacuum and dark. At $300\mathrm{K},$ electrical conductivities were found $800\mathrm{{S/m}}$ for $\mathsf{N i}_{3}(\mathsf{H I B})_{2}$ and $1300S/\mathrm{m}$ for $\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{H I B})_{2}$ . Temperature dependent profiles suggested that in both MOF conductivity correlates with temperature thus establishing the semiconducting nature. This was speculated near the interparticle junction (grain boundary). Further evidence of the metallic nature came from UPS spectroscopy wherein the fermi edges of both the compounds were indicative of metallic behaviour. This is the first report wherein porosity and metallic nature have been established.
Extensive industrial utilization of chlorine and bromine demands materials for efficient and effective storage due to the extreme toxicity. In this prospect, the previous group proposed that solid state materials may mitigate these challenges by formation of chemical interaction as physisorptive materials exhibit less effectiveness for lighter halogens. Their group synthesized a ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{II}})$ based MOF with formula $\mathrm{Co}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{BTDD}$ [277]. The choice of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ was motivated with the idea that storage may take place by redox recycling of metal ion from ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{II}})$ to ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{III}})$ and subsequent storage of $\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbf{B}}\ensuremath{\mathbf{r}}_{2}$ in their reduced form as $\mathbf{\nabla}[\mathbf{\nabla}]^{-}$ and $\mathsf{B r}^{-}$ by metal-halogen bond. They showed that such conversion is indeed possible and performed detailed investigation (Fig. 46a). Magnetic measurement studies revealed that the post exposure to $\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ the magnetic response changed form paramagnetic to diamagnetic with $\upmu_{\mathrm{eff}}$ reduced from $4.95~\upmu\up B$ per Co(II) $(S=3/2)$ to $0.15\upmu\mathrm{B}$ per ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{III}})$ Authors tried to extend this observation towards $\mathsf{F}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ but the experiment failed. Stability analysis via PXRD and FESEM revealed framework retention after oxidation which was itself an unprecedented report wherein ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathrm{III}})$ metal ion has been stabilized. Thermogravimetric analysis coupled with mass spectrometer confirmed that thermal energy was necessary for the elimination of adsorbed halogen and the release of halogen was in the form of ionized halogen products $^{\cdot35}\mathrm{Cl}^{+}$ , $^{37}\mathrm{Cl}^{+}$ , $^{158}\mathrm{{Br}_{2}^{+}}$ , $^{160}\mathrm{Br}_{2}^{+}$ etc. (Fig. 46b). Concomitant release and uptake were performed for three cycles and $\sim80\%$ release was observed. The release mechanism was probed further by DFT calculations which suggested release of adjacent halogen atom by radical mechanism. This report constitutes the first example of MOF showing capture and release of elemental $\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ .
In another seminal work by previous authors electrical conductivity was studied in detail. They illustrated how single crystal conductivity of mixed valence Fe(II)-Fe(III) based system can be tuned by 5 orders magnitude by varying the ratio of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ . They synthesized a neutral MOF with formula $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{0.67}\mathrm{BDT})_{3}{\cdot}17(\mathrm{H}_{2}0){\cdot}0.5$ $(\mathrm{iPrOH})$ (wherein $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDT}=5{,}5{\cdot}$ -(1,4-phenylene)bis(1H-tetrazole)) $\begin{array}{r}{(\mathsf{F e}_{2}(\mathsf{B D T})_{3})}\end{array}$ [278]. The asynthesized orange red crystals turned black upon exposure to air which was contributed to the oxidation of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ . Two-probe conductivity of single crystals were measured along the crystallographic $a$ -axis. The electrical conductivity value varied drastically before and after air exposure i.e $6(2)\times10^{-5}\mathrm{S/cm}$ (before air exposure) and $1.8(2)\mathrm{{S}/\mathrm{{cm}}}$ (post 30 days) for the latter case. This conductivity value lies among the highest in the domain of MOF exhibited by a single crystal. Arrhenius equation revealed the activation value of $160\mathrm{meV}$ which is appreciably low value. UV–Vis studies revealed a peak centered at $690\mathrm{nm}$ that was assigned to IVCT i.e Inter Valence Charge Transfer between $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ . Kubelka-Munk plot showed decrease in band gap after air exposure. The spin states were probed by DFT calculations and the computed band gap for the MOF matched well to the one found experimentally by UV–Vis spectroscopy. Density of states (DOS) calculations revealed that the valence band comprises of $\mathsf{N}$ and Fe centered orbitals confirming the charge transfer pathway through $(\mathrm{Fe-N-N-})_{\infty}$ chains. The same group in another report have probed whether iron element is unique in enhancing electrical conductivity [279]. They synthesized family of MOFs viz., $\mathsf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{DSBDC})(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}$ $\scriptstyle\mathbf{\big(}\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ ; ${\mathrm{H}}4{\mathrm{D}}{\mathsf{S B D C}}=2,5$ -disulfhy drylbenzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid), M(1,2,3-triazolate $)_{2}$ $\mathrm{(M=Mg^{2+}}$ , $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , $C0^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , $\operatorname{Cd}^{2+}$ ), $\mathsf{M}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{B T D D})(\mathsf{D M F})_{2}$ $\scriptstyle\mathbf{\backslash}M=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , $C0^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ; and $\mathbf{M}_{2}(\mathrm{DOBDC})(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}$ $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{M}\mathrm{g}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , $C0^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , $Z\mathrm n^{2+{\cdot}}$ ); $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{D}0\mathrm{B}\mathrm{D}\mathsf{C}=2,5$ -dihydroxyben zene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid; $\mathsf{D M F}=\mathsf{N},\mathsf{N}$ -dimethylformamide). Their studies highlighted the role of iron in improving the electrical conductivity of otherwise non-conducting framework. This reason was ascribed to the low activation energy observed in the case of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ high spin based MOFs. Because of the small effective nuclear charge and larger ionic radius the electrons in ${\mathsf{F e}}^{2+}{\mathsf{H}}S$ are highly energetic and can be promoted with ease. In few MOFs containing $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ in low spin state, the narrow band gap was explained on the basis of mixed valence states $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ via the oxidation of metal center upon air exposure. Formation of mixed valence state gives rise to mid gap states as calculated from projected density of states (PDOS) wherein electron can be placed via thermal activation. From the spin distribution, the calculations suggested that the spins are distributed equivalently throughout the metal centers thus facilitating inter-ion charge hopping.
Long and co-workers have also proposed a similar intervalence charge transfer [280]. They isolated a mixed valence framework via oxidation of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{tri})_{2}$ resulting in formation of $\mathrm{Fe(tri)}_{2}\mathrm{(BF_{4})}_{\times}$ $^{\prime}{\mathrm{tri}}=1,2,3$ -triazolate; $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}=0.09$ , 0.22, and 0.33). The compound with $\mathbf{X}=0.33$ showed the highest conductivity value $\upsigma=0.3$ (1) $S/{\mathrm{cm}}$ which was ascribed to the intervalence hopping. The proposed charge transfer pathway was confirmed by mossbauer spectroscopy in which the charge delocalised state of $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ was observed.
The same group in a very recent report have reported electrical conduction via electronic reduction [281]. Strategic reduction of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}(\mathsf{B D P})_{3}$ wherein $\mathrm{\Delta\cdotBDP}=1{,}4$ -benzenepyrazolate) was performed via potassium naphthalenide in tetrahydrofuran (THF) (Fig. 47a). They observed a decrease in surface area upon reduction which can be correlated to the inclusion of potassium ions in the framework with an overall formula ${\sf K}_{x}{\sf F e}_{2}({\sf B D P})_{3}$ $\begin{array}{r}{0\leq x\leq2,}\end{array}$ . The authors observed a distinct UV–Vis profile at ${\bf\nabla}\mathbf{X}=0.9$ which was corroborated to the formation of mid band gap states as described in above reports. The formation of mixed valence states influenced the electrical conductivity, with an increment of 10,000 fold along a crystallographic axis (Fig. 47b). Further, they have fabricated devices and performed FET (Field effect transistor) on a single crystal thus making pathway for development of novel compounds for electronic conduction.
N-donor based frameworks have shown an interesting prospect in the arena of electrocatalyst recently. Zhang and co-workers exhibited classical oxygen evolution reaction (OER) by utilizing classical H-ZIF (Zeolitic Imidazolate Framework) based MOF modulated by non 3D transition metals viz. $\mathsf{M o O}_{4}^{2-}/\mathsf{W O}_{4}^{2-}$ [282]. The authors observed an increase in the electrocatalytic activity post modulation. Highly water stable frameworks were synthesized with formula unit $\mathrm{\Gamma[Co_{4}(M O_{4})(e i m)_{6}]}$ $\mathbf{\dot{\mathbf{M}}}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{0}$ or W, Heim $=2$ - ethylimidazole). Mo-modulated framework showed the best OER performances of $1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ with an overpotential of $210\mathrm{mV}$ . The DFT calculations revealed that the non- $.3d$ metals modulate the electronic energy levels of the rate determining step and the activity of the compound was found to be comparable to known OER catalyst.
In another seminal work by Chen and co-workers, anion exchange was used as a platform to synthesize hydroxide terminated Co metal sites subsequently using it for electrocatalytic oxygen evolution reaction [283]. Triazole based MOF was synthesized viz. $\left[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C l})_{2}(\mathsf{b t t a})\right]$ (MAF-X27-Cl, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{ta}=1\mathrm{H},5\mathrm{H}$ -benzo(1,2-d:4 ,5-d’)bistriazole) which yielded the material via post-synthetic ion-exchange $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O H})_{2}(\mathsf{b t t a})]$ (MAF-X27-OH). An overpotential of $292\mathrm{mV}$ at $10.0\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ (1 M KOH) was observed and a drastic enhancement in the activity was observed. This was ascribed to the functionalization of MOF by metal-hydroxide bond and isotopic tracing experiments confirmed the role of hydroxide linkers in modulating the energy demands of the catalytic process.
Liao, Zhang and co-workers documented a detailed approach in understanding the cooperative effects of the active metal site and its neighboring ligands for photocatalytic ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ conversion [284]. The authors investigated six Co centered MOFs with varying coordination environment namely $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{\mu-C l})_{2}(\mathsf{b b t a})]$ (MAF-X27-Cl, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{ta}=1\mathrm{H},5\mathrm{H}$ -benzo (1,2-d:4,5-d0)bistriazole), $\left[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\upmu{-}\mathsf{O H})_{2}(\mathsf{b b t a})\right]$ (MAF-X27-OH), $\left[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{d o b d c})\right]$ (MOF-74-Co, $\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{dobdc}=2{,}5$ -dihydroxyl-1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid), $\vert C o_{2}(\upmu-$ $\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ (btdd)] (MAF-X27l-OH, $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{\mu-C l})_{2}(\mathsf{b t d d})]$ ] (MAF-X27l-Cl) and Co-ZIF-9 for photo-splitting ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ to CO. It was established that MOFs having $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{\mu-O H-}}$ non-structural ligands adjacent to open Co sites displayed superior catalytic performance both in terms of CO selectivity and TOF value. The best performance was observed for MAF-X27l-OH which demonstrated $98.2\%$ CO selectivity with a TOF value of $0.059s^{-1}$ for pure ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ at 1 atm preceding all reported heterogeneous catalysts. DFT calculations and isotope labeling experiments prevailed a bifunctional behavior of $\scriptstyle{\upmu-0}\mathrm{H}-$ ligands as H-bonding donor and local protonic source.
In another work Zhou, Zhang and co-workers explored the $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ sorption capacity of porous N-doped carbon derived from three isomorphic MOF precursors namely MAF-5(ANA, $38.6\%$ ), MAF-6 (RHO, $55.4\%$ and MAF-32 (qtz, $0\%$ ) having molecular formula $[Z\mathsf{n}$ $(\mathrm{eim})_{2}]$ ${\mathrm{.Heim}}=2$ -ethylimidazole) [285]. The so obtained porous carbon CMAF-5/CMAF-6/CMAF-32 prepared by heating under $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere had higher pore volume and surface areas than that of its precursor. All the N-doped porous carbon showed very good ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ uptake at 273 and $298\mathrm{K}$ while the uptake capacity of CMAF-6 at $273\mathrm{K}$ and 1 bar pressure was the highest amidst all porous carbon obtained from coordination polymers. Notably IAST calculation predicted that CMAF-5 and CMAF-32 had the best $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ selectivity among all porous carbons while mixed-gas breakthrough experiment $(15{:}85\ \mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ mixture) produced a ${C O}_{2}/{N}_{2}$ selectivity of 234 for CMAF-32.
Horcajada, Barea and co-workers tested an isoreticular ZnBDP_X MOF family assembled from 1,4-bis(1H-pyrazol-4-yl)-
2-X-benzene linker $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}B D P}\_{X}$ ; ${\big\vert}{\big\vert}{\big\vert}={\mathrm{H}}$ $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ , OH) as nanovessels for drug delivery [286]. Colloidal stability of ZnBDP_OH in oral simulated physiological environment presented satisfying structural and colloidal stability. Further these MOF nano-carriers were loaded with antitumor drugs such as mitroxantrone and $[\mathrm{Ru(p–cymene)Cl_{2}(p t a)}]$ (RAPTA-C) (where pta $^{\circ=1,3,5}$ -triaza-7- phospaadamantane) and impact of ligand functionality on encapsulation and delivery was further elucidated. The authors note that degree of encapsulation directly correlated with surface area while framework functionality had marked effect on the amount and kinetics of delivery. This was further validated when the authors detected ZnBDP_OH and $\mathrm{\ZnBDP\_NH_{2}}$ had slower kinetics while having higher degree of release compared to $\mathsf{Z n B D P\_N O}_{2}$ and ZnBDP_H.
Phase transition based studies of a inorganic-organic hybrid, a missing link in the field of MOFs was recently initialized by Kitagawa and co-workers [287]. This classical example for solid-liquid phase transition in coordination polymer was successfully investigated and has inspired further investigations towards development of this regime of application [288,289].
## 5. Conclusions and future perspectives
In summary, we have surveyed the recent advances and the current trends for applications studied using MOFs constructed from N-donor linkers, both neutral donor ligands and azolatebased systems. Although coordination polymers constructed out of neutral linkers such as pyridyl groups were proposed long while ago, MOFs built only from such donor groups have shown immense potential in recent times for a range of applications. Likewise, azolate based MOFs have shown tremendous promise as robust solidstate materials, which has resulted in them being sought for a wide array of applications. Thus the wide pool of compounds provides a database to screen design principles and form guidelines aimed at developing smart materials for future applications. In particular, given the research in terms of design strategies has been devoted to the more practical considerations such as stability, costeffectiveness, scalability, processability and toxicity. Although stand-alone N-donor based MOFs, especially neutral linker based, were initially proposed, less attention was paid owing to unsatisfactory delivery of the above parameters. But recent progress has resulted in reports were MOFs constructed out of such systems have shown great promise and effective applicability. A recent report by Chen and co-workers have demonstrated an approach to have azolate based MOF having both mesopore $(>20\mathring\mathrm{A})$ and micropore [290]. This report opened up a new domain of macroporousmicroporous MOFs wherein larger pore dimension along with stability from azolate framework leading to better prospects in MOFs. With regard to azolate-based systems, the field has progressed substantially, with greater focus on practical implementation over design approaches.
Apart from conventional applications in the domain of MOFs such as gas storage and separation, catalysis, the field has advanced to meet the demands of modern research such as fuel cell applications, clean energy and energy efficient capture of priority environmental pollutants. MOFs constructed out of both kinds of N-donor linkers have surged ahead keeping pace with these trends and have shown potential to compete with other classes of MOFs. We believe that retrospection of the field from the perspective of modern developments will contribute substantially to understanding of relevant parameters and factors required for practical applications.
